>But then it would have been a Pro 380 and not a DECmate at all.

Not exactly.

The DECmate III and III+ are a lot smaller than the Pro.  And if I built
it, it wouldn't run P/OS!

Yeah, an -11 processor would not a DECmate make, but you know what I mean.

'Course DEC did put a 78032 into basically the same box, ie the MicroVAX
2000, but it's not the same -- for starters there is barely enough room
for VMS V4.x on an RD32, let alone any space to get any work done (and
after about 5.2, just refuses to install at all in that little space, not
that that's stopped me in emergencies), and to add anything even
resembling peripherals you need a little pizza box strapped underneath
the thing.  The DECmate III+, on the other hand, sneaks an RD31 (aka
ST225), an RX33, CPU, memory, video output, coupla serial ports and 
internal space for two expansion cards into the little box. 

Stick a J11 into basically the same layout and you'd have have a nice 
little system, particularly if it could run _real_ RSX or RSTS.  Hey, I 
can dream.

I'm actually rather fond of the DECmate III[+] as a friendly reliable
canned system that dosn't dominate a user's desk and that a complete
neophyte can use and be productive with in about an hour -- I've brought
more than one outfit out of the 19th century with these things.

'course I'd never get away with such a thing now.  It has to be a huge
block with a panavision monitor and windows and a mouse (and mousepad)
with a desk of its own and it's gotta crash twice an day, eating an
hour's work each time or no-one will believe it's worth anything.

<sigh>

