>
>
>Hello
>
>
>I have a Digital vr299 monitor connected to a vax 3100. Lately, the
>monitor switches itself off very often (the screen pops out; the green
>light on the front panel stays on). When the screen switches off, I
>hear a click, perhaps from a relais. Can it be the energy saving mode
>is defective? If so, does anybody know how to fix this (or bypass the
>energy saving mechanism)? (Ps. the pictures in the manual indicate a
>monitor cable with mouse and keyboard cables attached to it to
>indicate user activity; the cable of my monitor does not have such
>connections)
>
>
>Thanks
>
>
>Wout van Wezel
>w.m.c.van.wezel@bdk.rug.nl

I bought a VR290 the other day that did the same thing.  I found out that the 
HV lead from the HV transformer was loose.  I had to take out about 100 screws 
to get to it, and it still appears loose, either by a bad design or age.  
Anyway, I pushed it in all the way and viola!  a working monitor.

NOTE:  This High Voltage wire I mention is VERY VERY DANGEROUS.  It runs 
anywhere from 20,000 to 27,000 volts!  DO NOT DISSASSEMBLE ANY MONITOR UNLESS 
YOU KNOW WHAT YOU ARE DOING, and even then THINK!

My two cents worth




