In article <52dl1i$22k@pheidippides.axion.bt.co.uk>, thomasgd@boat.bt.com (Greg Thomas) writes:
> A simple question ... Do Digital still supply Digital-style PC keyboards 
> - i.e. with F1-F20 and PF1-PF4 keys, etc. (prefereably UK versions, but US would 
> do). They would need Windows 3.11 and Windows NT drivers. I had an old LK250, 
> but that got trashed a while ago.

Yes.  You want the LK450, which is a perfectly beautiful keyboard that
happens to also plug into a PS2-sized keyboard jack.  I'm using it on
an Alphastation.  For reasons which are unclear to me, the LK450-AA also
has the WPS keys silkscreened on it, but they are relatively innocuous
and easy to ignore, except for that one hour a year I'm stuck in A1.

It also has a "British pound" sign silkscreened over the 3 key (in addition
to the NA-traditional "sharp" sign).  I'm not sure what other differences
there are in UK vs. US keyboards; perhaps the z and s keys are swapped? :-)

Digital has a newer version of this keyboard which is even cheaper; it's
called the LK46W.  I don't like it very much; it has even less play the
the LK450 (and coming from a VT100 keyboard, that's not much play) and
a cheaper feel.

