In article <52d3u3$dtn@access1.digex.net>, rdd@access1.digex.net (R. D. Davis) writes:
|>Yesterday, I missed the UPS truck attempting to deliver an RQDX3, but
|>this afternoon I got my RQDX3! :-)
|>
|>There's just one problem, however - my MicroVAX-II won't boot after
|>installing the RQDX3. :-(
|>
|>I've tried inserting the RQDX3 into both the AB and CD parts of slot
|>No. 4, but it still doesn't seem to be working.  The system does pass
|>all of its self-tests, and I get to:
|>
|>    Tests completed.
|>
|>    Loading system software.
|>
|>    2..
|>  ?4C DEVINACT, DUA0
|>  ?06 HLT INST
|>      PC = 00000EE6
|>  Failure.
|>  >>>
|>


If this is a BA123 box, then the RQDX2 should sit in the AB portion, if it is
a BA23 box, slot 4 is Q/Q not Q/CD so you need a grant card in the "AB" bit of
slot 4.

Looks to me like your MicroVAX can see your RQDX3 but your RQDX3 cannot see your
device. If this is an RD disk, check the cabling and then check it again - on
the BA23 box at least it is very easy to have a cable that is not quite fully
pushed in either on the drive or the BA23 bulkhead.



|>If I try any of the tests, such as "T 50", "T 0", etc. I get the
|>"Failure."  message.  The floppy drive indicators don't light up
|>either to indicate that it's trying to boot from a floppy.
|>

I don't think the KA630 understands these commands.

|>Should the RQDX3 be in a different slot?  Do I need to change any of
|>the jumper settings?  Here's how the jumpers are set on this RQDX3:
|>

The RQDX3 generally goes in the last slot (i.e. electrically furthest from 
the CPU). But this is really mostly for performance reasons - to stop it hogging 
the bus. It should work in any Q-22 slot. There must be no break in grant
continuity before it though.

I don't have an RQDX3 manual handy here, but I can check the details if
you still can't get it working. Send me mail if you need the settings and
I'll look it up.

