Sweet, although a little short on slots...

I have a bizarre 3rd party box lying around which is intended to be a 
portable system.  Unfortunately, the way it is made portable is to have 
fold-out metal handles on both sides so two people can carry it.

It contains an 8-slot dual-width backplane with an LSI-11/2 processor, 
DLV11-J, 28kw memory, and a couple of A/D & D/A cards.  The rear panel 
has banana-plug sockets for the analogue ports; the front panel contains 
a TU58 drive and distribution panel for three of the DLV11-J ports.  The 
fourth port, the console port, is wired to the TU58; the CPU uses the 
boot mode of the TU58 to get its marbles together, and lacks boot ROMs.

(In boot mode, the TU58 reads the tape and spits the contents down the 
serial port in clear-text (ie not using RSP), 7 characters at a time, 
which corresponds nicely to the size of a Micro-ODT command.  The CPU us 
strapped to halt on startup.)

