barlin@decus.org.au (Peter BARLIN) writes:

>I couldn't agree more about this config.
>THIS is what DEC should have flogged as a home machine and would have been 
>about the size of a BA11-M.(once configured similar system wih KDJ11-a,MXV11-b,
>KLESI in one of these backplanes powered by a H780-b an connected to RC25. 
>The RC25 was the Archilles heel unfortunately).

Yes, RC25 is a pain (I have two on one of my machines... but I'm working
on getting all my backups off of them since they are so unreliable)

Years ago, I picked up a TechMate, which was a system designed for use
by Dec Field Service for debugging systems (mostly pdp-11's).  They
never made it as a real tool since they were in carrying cases which
were too heavy.  However, the system consists of an LSI backplane (I
think it was 4 slot, but it might have been 8... I haven't had mine
opened for some time).  It originally had an 11/2, but I fairly quickly
upgraded it to a KDJ11-A.  It has two MXV11-A's, for memory, boot code
and the serial lines - one for console (a built-in VT100 with a tiny
5" screen and membrane keyboard), one for the system device (TU58) and
two for printer and/or modems.  The box is reinforced metal and there
is very little room... but I always thought about making some room for
something like an RD32 and using an RQDX3...

Other than this system, I have a system which consists of two BA11-SA
(shoebox) boxes, with a KDJ11-A, MXV11-B, RQDX3 and DELQA.  The four
boards are too much for one power supply, which is why I use two boxes.
I have jumpered the backplanes for 22-bit and use a 22-bit backplane
jumper (BCV1B?) between them  I have an RD54 and paired RX33's in
leprachaun boxes attached to it.  Physically the smallest system I
have, but still in four boxes...

