In article <1996Sep22.105717.16440@decus>, barlin@decus.org.au (Peter BARLIN) writes:
> Interesting, I was informed that the KDJ11-a(m8192) wasn't compatible with 
> the MXV11-A boot roms.I assume that you haven't rewired backplane for 22bits.

  There's "supported" and then there's compatible. Manufacturing Engineering
decreed that in order for a system to be eligible for support, it had to have
cache diagnostics in the boot ROMs. The -A boot ROMs are too small and too
old, and so nobody ever bothered to update 'em.

  The MXV11-B2, OTOH, was *huge* by comparison and got the code.

  The KDJ11-A was always intended as an OEM-only part, and many OEM's were 
noted for only buying the CPU board, so DEC never really expended any effort
on these ROMs (which is why they can't boot a TK25 or MU devices, though I
fixed both of those [Hi, Steve!; Hi, Jeff!]).

