The RC25 is an awkward shape to put into such a box, and the media was
too expensive.  DEC put it into the VAX 11/725, with mixed results.  (All
the 725s I've seen or heard of have been in Aus; I've never seen even a
mention of the beast anywhere else. I wonder why that is.)  The 725, with
its hex-wide backplane, RC25 & PSU went together like one of those block
packing puzzles.

But going with a full-height 5.25" ST506-interface MFM disk & an RX50, I
reckon a pdp11 could have been got into a PC-sized box.  Maybe something
like the Rainbow/Pro/DECmateII configuration, with the drives above a
two-slot hex-wide backplane with a KDF11, RQDX1, and an MXV11-B, with a
quad space free for MSV11 or other modules.  Three hex slots would have
produced a serious threat to the pdp11/23....  Such a beast could have
been produced cheaply in the early 80s, in time to take a swing at the
IBM PC, and could have had a long life, with upgrades to a J11 processor,
better disk controllers, denser parts etc.

Someone from Sweden posted a while ago about how he converted a VT103 (an
LSI11/2 in a 4-slot quad 16-bit Qbus backplane housed in a VT100 chassis)
first into a 22-bit 11/23, then an 11/73, and finally into a MicroVAX
II.

And of course if DEC had put a DCJ11 into the DECmate III+ instead of 
that stupid 6120.... 8-)

