>I think that's a bit harsh.  If you're talking about the PDP-11/40 
>mentioned in the subject, then yeah, it's a pretty chunky bit of 
>hardware, but pdp11s don't need to be that big.  Certainly, the BA23 
>boxed machines are eminently shippable.  Not _very_ cheap, but doable, 
>and that gets a usable system (BYO console terminal) in the one box.

The smallest PDP11 that I own is actually quite easily shipable - in fact 
it was sent to me by normal parcel post. It's built in a somewhat unusual 
DEC mounting box called a BA11-V, which is the same size as the tabletop 
TU58 (they were designed to stack, and there's even a power outlet on the 
BA11-V to power a special TU58), and which contains a switch-mode PSU and 
a 4-slot dual-height Q-bus backplane, which I _suspect_ is 18-bit 
addressing only.

The particular machine that I have contains the following cards : 

SBC21 (Falcon) CPU which special boot ROMs and PLAs
MSV11 32K word MOS memory
DPV11 sync serial card
DRV11 parallel port.

This configuration was known in the UK as a 'york box' and was used to 
link machines lacking a sync serial port to the X25 network. I am told 
that they were quite common at one time, but I've not seen that many of them

AFIAK there's no reason why you couldn't add the 22 bit address lines to 
the backplane and put an 11/73 CPU card in there.

>If you just want the CPU box, some of the 11/03 and 11/23 systems came in

The problem with any of the Q-bus machines is that they contain a lot of 
custom silicon that's very difficult to find replacements for. The older 
Unibus machines tend to be a lot of standard 74xxx (or 74Sxxx etc) TTL, 
which you can still find. Of course _finding_ the defective chip out of 
the several thousand in the system can prove entertaining.


>fairly small and comparitively light boxes, as did the 11/05, and the
>latter has a real front panel so you don't _need_ a console terminal. 8-)

Having once carried an 11/05 on a train, I can quite honestly say that 
they're not that light. On the other hand, they are portable - just ;-)

The problem with shipping an 11/40 (the machine that started this thread)
is that the 11/40 nearly always comes in the 21" BA11-F cabinet (yes, I
know there was a version in the BA11-K, but it's not common!). This
cabinet has separate PSUs in the rack, and thus is decidedly non-trivial
to dismount and reasssemble - if you do intend shipping one of these in
pieces, the same person should be present at both ends, so he can remember
what went where - and make plenty of notes as it comes apart.  Then, of
course, you do need a 6' rack at the 'receiving' end, and these are not
trivial to ship, even empty. Shipping one that contains an 11/40 would be
even harder IMHO. 

IMHO, the ideal home-PDP11 (if such a thing exists) is the 11/44. The CPU 
is a single 10.5" box that's _just_ liftable by one person when fully 
assembled. The PSU is internal, and almost all the logic uses standard 
ICs, so it's easy to repair. There's enough space in the cabinet for 13 
extra expansion slots ( 1 single system unit, 1 double system unit), and 
there are 2 serial ports (for the console and the TU58) in the CPU board 
set. And, of course, you get 22 bit addressing.


>I've often mused about putting a KDJ11-A, some memory, a boot/console 
>card and an RQDX3 into a double-width backplane, and mounting it inside 
>one of those shoeboxes designed to take a couple of 5.25" form factor 
>disks along with an RD32 disk and possibly an RX33.  Sort of a 
>pdp11-in-a-brick.

I once considered making a pocket PDP11. The thing would have used a 
4-slot dual-height Q-bus backplane, containing a 11/23 CPU (I didn't have 
any 11/73's at that time), a 3rd party 64K word/2 serial port card, an 
Emulex SCSI card, and possibly a Plessey 8" floppy controller. That lot 
would then be cabled up to a battery pack (in my other pocket ;-)), a 
small SCSI drive, and a pocket terminal.


