In article <51vr90$bue@globalnews.globalvision.net>,
mclark@c235.globalvision.net (Mitchell Clark) writes:
:I have an old VAX 2000 but need to run a new application that needs a
:mouse (or other pointer). Any suggestions ???

  Suggestions?  Sure... Don't start a land war in Asia.  (I love
  ambiguous questions.  :-)

  Seriously, The MicroVAX 2000 and VAXserver 2000 series boxes do
  not (directly) support a pointing device.

  Some serial terminals and most (all?) X Windows terminals support
  a mouse.  (The serial terminals that have mouse support typically
  require specific device support within the application program.)

  The VAXstation 2000 is a workstation that can -- given sufficient
  physical memory -- run DECwindows.  The VAXstation 2000 system
  configuration usually (always?) included a mouse.  (The other
  windowing system these systems were sold with was VWS -- this
  windowing system predates X Windows.)

  You'll want to see what sort of pointing-device support is present
  in the new application.  If, on the other hand, you are _writing_
  this new application and have a MicroVAX 2000, you'll likely want
  to upgrade to a VAXstation or AlphaStation, and you'll want to use
  the X Windows support.

  If `cheap' is a requirement, upgrade to a (used) VAXstation 3100 or
  a new or used VAXstation 4000 series box -- you'll get a mouse, you
  will be able to add more memory, and you'll get a SCSI interconnect.

