Subject: SUMMARY: [Q] Can a VS2000 boot over its AUI ethernet connector?
Date: 1996/09/23
Message-ID: <1996Sep23.153103.1@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 184907411
sender: news@unixhub.SLAC.Stanford.EDU
references: <1996Sep19.090429.1@sldb3.slac.stanford.edu>
organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms


Last week I posted asking:

In article <1996Sep19.090429.1@sldb3.slac.stanford.edu>, 
    				fairfield@sldb3.slac.stanford.edu writes:
>       Question: Has anyone ever successfully booted a VS2000 over its
>     	--------  AUI Ethernet connection?
[...]

        We had recently  replaced  all  our  existing Thinwire (10base2)
    cable  plant  with UTP (10baseT), which necessitated  switching  the
    VS2000's from their BNC Thinwire connectors to their AUI  connectors
    (and  from  there  to a cute little AUI-to-UTP transceiver).  Having
    done so, none of the VS2000's could boot.

        Most people who responded asked  whether the little black slider
    switch on the back of the VS2000 had been moved to the "B" position,
    where  "A"  selects  the BNC and "B" selects the AUI (sort  of  like
    asking, "did you make sure it's plugged in?"  :-).   The  answer  is
    "yes", the switch is in the correct position, which should have been
    apparent  from  the  rest  of  my  description, that is, that MOP/DL
    packets were seen be sent over  the  AUI,  and were even received by
    the VAX servers, but that the timing was wrong (48 MOP/DL packets in
    a  row  with no wait-for-response in between).  Also, >>>T 50  shows
    the NI device "good".

        A few people (two?) responded saying  they had, indeed, used the
    AUI    with    VS2000's.     In   private   e-mail,    John    Stott
    <jpstott@src.wisc.edu> said, "Some systems care  about  whether  the
    SQE is enabled on the transceiver."  This is something I'd suspected
    but was  hoping  someone  could  answer  more  definitively.   Brian
    Tillman  <tillman_brian@si.com>  said  in e-mail that he  was  using
    exactly the same setup as I outlined (by which I take it he's  using
    a  CenterCOM  transceiver  on  the  VAXstation's  AUI  to convert to
    10baseT).  Also in  e-mail,  Allen Rueter <Allen@mir.wustl.edu> said
    that   he'd   successfully  used  the  VS2000's  AUI's,  but  in   a
    configuration like AUI <-> transceiver <-> delni <-> transceiver <->
    h4005.  With all that "other" equipment in there, it  seemed  to  me
    that,  especially  on  older  DEC  stuff, one or more of the devices
    might have had SQE ("heartbeat")  enabled,  but that _seemed_ to not
    be an option for me...

        Well,  in  looking  over/handling  one  these  little  CenterCOM
    transceivers  this morning, I happened to notice an almost illegible
    (to my old eyes!) set of markings on the side, just above a recessed
    slot, wherein there was a tiny slide switch (use the end of a  paper
    clip  to  move it).  The legend said, "SQE TEST SWITCH (HEARTBEAT)",
    and the default switch position is  "OFF".  OK, says I, let's change
    it  to  "ON".  Viola!  Sure enough, the VS2000 booted like a  champ,
    just like in the "old days" of Thinwire.

        So the solution is: 
              
        VAXstation 2000's MUST HAVE SQE ENABLED on the transceiver.
        ===============   =====================        ===========

        Curiously, just _after_  I'd  found  this  solution,  I received
    e-mail  from  Mike  Monscheuer  <Monscheuer@T-Online.de>  who  said,
    "Perhaps  too  simple:  The  transceiver's  switch  setting  as  for
    heartbeat  and/or  SQ?"  Yep,  that was it Mike.  I didn't even know
    that the transceiver _had_ a switch for SQE before this morning.

        So thanks to all who  posted  or  e-mailed!  It looks like we're
    back in business.  :-)

        -Ken
