Larry Kilgallen <kilgallen@eisner.decus.org> wrote:
>In article <526f2f$mr7@fido.asd.sgi.com>, gainer@gainer.engr.sgi.com (Patrick Gainer) writes:
>
>> I guess I'll have to begin championing two modifications to the
>> general TPC benchmarks now:
>> 
>> 	1, Full disclosures MUST be done only with hardware and
>> 	   software which is currently generally availaable.
>
>I do not think vendors would let this through the TPC,
>since their interest is in selling the latest stuff.

Taking a somewhat more cynical view, I'll agree with you but for different
reasons. Vendors will not allow this through the TPC because it will
force them to benchmark code they actually sell in their products 
rather than "not yet shipping" code. My first modification has
nothing to do with benchmarking the latest stuff. You just have to be
able to actually buy what is being benchmarked.

>> 	2, Full disclosures MUST include the total count of all
>> 	   components used in the benchmark, INCLUDING all failed
>> 	   components. 
>
>Vendors will just start the benchmark over until they get a run
>with zero drive failures. 

You're missing my point. With some of the larger TPC benchmarks into 
the 1000 disk drive range, disk failures start to become a very large 
problem. My point was intended to force companies to publish the number 
of parts which break during a typical TPC benchmark. I expect this
would go over like a lead balloon also simply because nobody wants to
imply huge systems like these ever experience failures.

Pat
Note that these opinions are mine and not necessarily those of SGI.



