>
>But this doesn't stop a malignant company like Microsoft or Netscape to
>introduce "extensions", say callable library routines that do something
>fancy.  For an applet using these routines to run, the library must be
>present, which means you must be using some piece of proprietary
>software for your environment.  Any test suite _not_ using these extras,
>will of course run perfectly. 
>
>Or do Sun/JavaSoft force licencees to not extend Java in this manner?
>Am I missing something obvious here?  And when do we get separately
>developed Java-clones?
>
>~kzm
>

Java like any language will grow in the sense that libraries of callable
functions will be produced for it. However this will not affect the core
language. Also since JAVA is an object oriented language such functions
will be blackboxes ie can be totally replaced by a function with the 
same interface and producing the same output but implemented in a different
manner. Hence if someone produces a must proprietary function for Java
it can easily be implemented by other companies or users.

However Java will not probably be the only choice for writing Java applets
and applications. Java is just a language - the key thing is the Java virtual
machine. I can easily see other compilers JAVA-FORTRAN, JAVA-PASCAL ,
JAVA-COBOL etc being created which produce Java bytecodes to run on the Java
virtual machine.

