In article <527csj$29h@fido.asd.sgi.com>, gainer@gainer.engr.sgi.com 
(Patrick Gainer) writes...

>You're missing my point. With some of the larger TPC benchmarks into 
>the 1000 disk drive range, disk failures start to become a very large 
>problem. My point was intended to force companies to publish the number 
>of parts which break during a typical TPC benchmark. I expect this
>would go over like a lead balloon also simply because nobody wants to
>imply huge systems like these ever experience failures.

I should admit that I'm a SPEC person, I do i/o and tpc only under grave
duress.  But in speaking to my colleagues who are familiar with the 1TB+
configurations we have around the lab, the word is that indeed one gets
a number of disk failures when first building a large configuration, but
that after the system has been used for a while, failures become very
rare - even on the very largest configurations, you might go months
without needing to access the backups.

So if the proposal is to make TPC more "realistic" by defending more
vigorously against drive failures, I think you could measure the
strength of the defense, but I don't think you could realistically 
count the failures.  There just aren't very many, or at least not very
many by the time you're ready for the audit stage.

