(correctly because I hadn't declared it) interpreting the floating point 'bit
pattern', returned by the function, as an _integer_ . It was then doing a
calculation similar to yours, so it converted the 'integer' to double and gave
the unexpected result.

  Once I had tracked down the cause, I wondered why I hadn't received and
'implicit function type' whinge from the compiler. It took me several hours
to track the problem down to a buglet in the types.h standard header. Near
the top was a "#pragma __nostandard" but there was no corresponding
"#pragma __standard" at the bottom to turn the warnings back on. So, anything
that included types.h (and this tended to be indirectly, via X11 headers for
example) had the warnings switched off ! This was fixed by DEC C 5.3 I believe.

  Short version: Have you included math.h ? :-)

