After a letter from dsr@lns598.lns.cornell.edu (Daniel S. Riley), I
decided to use -std1 to compile the program, and indeed, it suddenly
worked.

Inspection of <math.h> (which I *had* included) reveiled that
functions are declared without their prototype, unless the
preprocessor macro _NO_PROTO is *undefined*.

What reason anybody can have to not included prototypes is beyond me,
but now we are apparently forced to use -std all the time. However,
since the compiler in default mode does recognize prototypes, there
seems to be no reason not to include them.

Perhaps the next releases can leave _NO_PROTO undefined???

