Whether a given DEC workstation supports both the SRM (VMS and Unix)
console and the ARC (Windows NT) console depends on what model you
have.  The PC 150/DEC 2000 line has enough flash ROM to hold both
consoles.  So does the AlphaStation 250.  Many Alphastations do not.

If your Alpha workstation is not from DEC, I know at least one other
manufacturer proudly proclaimed that they had not skimped on this
part of the design, but I would doubt that is a uniform answer in
that space either.

If you are unlucky, please complain.  Somehow the DEC workstation
designers got it into their heads that nobody would want to switch
back and forth between operating systems, and they never heard any
reaction to the contrary.

If you are not in the US, that does not make your opinion less
valued.  DEC does 50% of their business outside the US.

==========

Now there are ways to switch the console code back and forth,
and I have heard there are methods to run the console code from
a floppy, but I feel DEC is being quite shortsighted to cripple
the machine due to the cost of extra Flash ROM.

Larry Kilgallen



