am not trying to advertise for the K460, but just have a technical
discussion on what data there is.

>>The reason the K460 is not much cheaper in terms of $/tpmC is
>>due to mainly pricing games played by DEC and Sun.  In addition,
>>the HP disclosure uses ALL HA disk arrays (running RAID5 and
>>RAID1) whereas the DEC and Sun use only arrays for the log device.
>>The HP disclosure represents a "real word configuration".  No 
>>real customer would run in a JBOD configuration with 770GB of
>>disk storage!
>
>Are you saying that the TPC "acid tests" (which require data integrity
>across system hardware failures) are not sufficiently real world?
>
Yes.  Having to rely on a tape backup for recovery in the event
of losing a disk is not good.  Tapes are usually used in extreme
situations (earthquake, fire, human error, etc) and not really
intended for a disk failure that happens every 2weeks -> 1month.
For small setups (say < 30 disks) you can probably get by without
having redundancy in the disks, but for the setups in the 300->1000
disk range you just can't live without some sort of RAID.

I've heard rumors that for the DEC 4-node TPC-C number (30390 tpmC at
$305/tpmC) a disk went bad every few days.  This number had about 750
disk drives priced (I'm not sure how many were actually used).  DEC
was able to recover by storing a backup copy of the database on RAID5
arrays.  When a disk went bad they just replaced the disk and dd'ed
the copy back onto the "test" disks.

I claim the only reason you would go to these measures would to get
a lower $/tpmC.  No real customer would buy this setup (especially
in an OPS setup where they are hopefully concerned about more than
performance!).   HP has taken the high-ground and is publishing
numbers above 10,000 tpm with RAID.

So bottom line is the $/tpmC may not be as "stellar" as the performance,
but overall I believe HP has delivered a more reasonable configuration
and not a benchmark special hardware configuration.
    
>I think your numbers show that the PA8000 has some fine strengths, but
>your marketing department chose to describe those strengths in a way
>that showed a substantial disregard for the rules of TPC, less than
>complete information about your competitors, and an utter lack of
>clarity for the terms "class", "midrange", etc.
>
Other than the Aberdeen thing and the 8200 was there anything else?
I was upset about the 8200 number also, I thought they ought to just
go after the 8400 :)!

As for less than complete information everyone does that!  Everyone
wants to show how great their product is so they use comparisons that
they think will show it off in the correct light.  I'm sure your marketing
won't have the K460 on any slides with the 8400 -- that would be silly
because they would show how their high-end is only 15% faster than
our midrange.  It's all selective marketing.  I totally agree that
it is bogus at times, but that's why I'm an engineer and not in 
marketing. 

Just for the record, the K460 is a mid-range class box.  It does not
have nearly the bus (in terms of MB/sec) or the I/O capacity of a
DEC 8400, but it does have a lot going for it (120MHz bus, fast
I/O slots, compact design, low cost, high performance).  I'm sure
there are some benchmarks that an 8400 will just kill a K460, but
for HP's server market it has all of the qualities that we need
to be successful (great TPC-C, SAP, SFS, and specWEB performance
in a midrange box).  

>In any case, thanks for your response.  I appreciate it.
>
Hey, it's always good looking at these boxes from the technical side
and seeing how all of the boxes work on different workloads.
Thanks for pointing out that our marketing message was a little
weak.  Maybe our marketing guys can take note

--Chris
Again, my own opinions, and not HP's.





