>Not a stupid question.  While I don't claim to have a handle on all of what

Indeed.  When one has questions, one should ask others about them.
Questions are not stupid.

>In the March 1995 issue of DEC Professional there is a discussion of
>an operating system called TSX that runs on an intel platform.  It
>has logicals, and symbols, ala vms.  Lexicals are even available.

There's a free, somewhat limited, trial version of TSX-32 available
via anonymous FTP.  Note that TSX-32 requires 8MB of RAM to run.  What
I don't understand is why it requires so much memory on an IBM type PC
when I can run TSX-Plus (the PDP-11 version) on my PDP-11 with 4MB of
memory, and it can run in much less memory than that as well.

>It has been around about 20 years, according to the article, and was
>initially designed it for a PDP-11 that was running RT-11 a single
>user OS.  He wanted to use this expensive (at the time) machine for
>multiple users.

It's still, from what I can tell, not as much like VMS as is
RSX-11(M(+)).  Which came first, TSX or RSX?  (yes, I know, TSX was
not a DEC product).  Note that although RT-11 can be viewed as a
single user OS, multiple users can use programs on multiple terminals.

>It is NOT vms, but appears to walk and talk much like vms.

Note that the user interface is much different from that of VMS.

>The company that owns/distributes(?) it is:
>S&H Computer Systems Inc.
>Phone: (615) 327-3670
>Fax: (615) 321-5959
>BBS: (615) 320-1820
>E-mail: 71333.27@compuserve.com

They also have an sandh.com e-mail address.  Their web page is at
http://www.sandh.com.  You probably won't find much about TSX+ for
PDP-11s there, however.  From what I can tell, it seems like a nice
alternative to MS-Windoze, but is much more expensive than FreeBSD or
Linux - be forwarned that it does have lots of MS-DOSisms atached to
it.

Don't expect TSX-32 to look like VAX/VMS when you use it, however - it
will appear more like a multi-user version of MS-DOS, with more
commands and features. Similarly, TSX+ has the basic appearance of
RT-11, with added features.

Speaking of PCisms and VMS, there also used to be a version of Wendin
DOS that had a VAX/VMS-like user interface, but ran MS-DOS programs.
It was basically a multi-user, multi-tasking, MS-DOS replacement, and
could run on a PC with an 8088 CPU and not too much memory (very
slowly!).  There were also MD-DOS-like and UNIX-like interfaces
available.  I think that it's creator got sucked up into the world of
Microsoft and now works as one of the demons of the AntiLogic.

Lastly, there's also a DCL-like command.com replacement available
somewhere on the 'net via anonymous FTP.

