|>
|>>>The LED "countdown" display on the back of the chasis didn't light up
|>>>at all, and a terminal connected to the console port doesn't display
|>>>anything.  When powered up, the four LEDs on the back of the CPU board
|>
|>Oops... I made a mistake.  Initially, I counted only 4 LEDs, but upon
|>a second examination, there are 5 LEDs.  It appears that the top one 
|>may be green.
|>

If the board is in a horizontally mounted BA23 (i.e. the KA630 is horizontal,
component side up) then the LEDs are:

                o         o         o         o         o
              POWER       8         4         2         1



Take the Nautilus console board (M7553) out and check the wiring for the
external LED and console port.

There are two cables from the KA630. Again assuming it is mounted horizontally
the smaller connector on the left should have the cable at the bottom of
the connector (right next to the KA630) with the stripe on the left. The 
slightly larger connector (just to the right of the other one) should have the
cable at the top with the stripe on the right (i.e. "upside-down").

These cables go to the insert on the back panel (with the battery and the
switches). Assuming you have this mounted vertically and the right way up both
cables will have the red sripe uppermost, with the larger connector being
above the smaller connector.

If you get this wrong it is possible to have a system which works but which
won't talk to the console terminal.

Once you get it working you'll probably find it is sitting there unable to
boot from anything - but at least you'll know it's alive :-)



