In article <borsom-1609961043110001@192.0.2.1>, borsom@netcom.com (Douglas H. Borsom) writes:
|> In the mean time, for benighted fools who want very fast hardware
|> that runs a 64-bit OS, (your choice, UNIX or VMS)  or NT (and coming down
|> the road, 64-bit NT), there's Alpha.

Which kind of brings up an interesting question, which is probably way
off topic, so let's at least drop comp.benchmarks :-)  I have read
the hype, listened to Microsoft, and contemplated over the expectations
of IS managers and there are still some things I don't understand.

So what is to prevent NT from having the same sort of fragmented
evolution as UNIX ?  It has taken much effort on the part of hardware
OEMs, ISV's, and users to standardize enough of UNIX to be
interesting.  So much of UNIX's value today is derived from all the
different variants and subsequent standardizations/re-integrations
(SVR4, SPEC 1170, CDE, etc) and this did not come for free.

As vendors add interesting "features" such as MPP, clustering,
64-bitness, high availibility, fault tolerance, etc, it seems a naive
view that NT will remain completely ubiquitous though all of this - ie
the middleware and vendor extensions become more valuable than the
common NT code base.

A strict technology ownership model by Microsoft, which will be called
proprietary by competitors and critics (and rightfully so), will limit
the unbounded exploration that has made UNIX what it is today.
Microsoft's strength is desktop applications and the O/S is simply an
enabler for those applications.  It is too much a leap of faith to
believe that this model could ever build a robust operating environment
for the diversity of the enterprise.

A limited partnership model (Microsoft and select friends) is the most
dangerous scenario where there are multiple competitive implementations
that mostly work alike but may differ on some basic functionality.
Does the Berkeley Standard Distribution and ATT System V ring a bell
(no pun intended).

An open specification based approach to NT seems totally out of the
question, I mean this is still Microsoft we are talking about :-)

10 years from now, when everything runs Java on a stack based
microprocessor this won't be much of an issue, but for now it does make
food for thought.......

Bob
