Boris V. Khattatov wrote:
> 
> A friend of mine (who does not have access to newsgroups) ran into the
> following problem: he needs to attach a 5.25'' floppy drive to an
> AlphaServer 400 running OpenVMS. The Alpha has FD controller on an ISA
> slot. ISA devices are not
> capable of being probed for configuration information by OpenVMS. A user
> must enter
>  ISA  information manually using the ISA configuration utility (ISACFG).
> This utility is a part of SRM console. ISACFG allows to modify an entry
> in config database for adding new device. Database has entry for a 3.5"
> drive but not for a 5.25" drive. If anybody knows how to configure a
> 5.25" drive with ISACFG, please respond to
> 
>                         vgasa.voronezh.su
>                                         or
>                         cheget.acd.ucar.edu
> 
> Thanks a *LOT* !
> 


  Well, let us rather say that OpenVMS does not probe the ISA bus looking for known
  devices.  ISA devices are configured based on the presence of a ISACFG HANDLE that
  matches a device ID in the ISA bus configuration logic.  Alternatively, the user
  could define the ISA device in a file called ISA_CONFIG.DAT (see the .template)
  for this.

  But this doesn't really matter.  The first question to ask is - does the user have
  a driver for this controller?  If they don't, then no amount of configuration will
  help.  If they do - then they should use ISACFG to allocate any resources, such as
  the IRQ (the *most* important thing is the IRQ).  But this information will *not*
  be used to configure the driver.  They need to edit ISA_CONFIG.TEMPLATE and define
  the device.  See the directions in the file.  There is no way in V6.* or V7.0 to
  get VMS to autoconfigure devices that are not already known.  ISA_CONFIG.DAT is
  the exception, and it's messy as it both describes and defines a specific
  instance of a device - the ISACFG utility only reserves the resources.

  In V7.1, we are completely changing the way OpenVMS finds it's "known" devices.
  All devices (including the ones directly supported by DEC) will be described in
  a set of data files in plain text.  Adding configuration support will then be
  simply a process of adding about 6 lines to a data file, and using ISACFG to 
  provide a HANDLE and allocate any resources.  ISA_CONFIG.DAT will be obsoleted
  in a subsequent release of OpenVMS (post V7.1).

  Seamless, integrated, finally plug-and-play.



