devine@indigo.ie (Alan Devine) writes:

> I had initially thought about a Pentium but then I saw an
> Advertisement for an Alpha 166 21066 (including 24mb ram, 340mb hard
> disk and numerous extra's, did NOT include a monitor) for $800.

This is the UDB/Multia, it is a fairly complete box, with sound and
PCMCIA and ethernet integrated.  I have one here running Linux, and I'm
pretty satisfied with its performance, however...

> Although I have never used an Alpha I have heard they are very
> powerful.

Several different chips exist, and they vary in computational power--a
lot.  The multia is using a low end 166Mhz chip.

> I know that to run MS-DOS programs I'll have to run them thru an
> emulator and this is slower, but is actually that much slower,
> compared to running them through a Pentium 150?

I think the answer to that would be a resounding ``yes''.  Even with the
FX!32's capability to produce native applications, I think a P150 would
be a better choice if performance is the issue.

> Do programs wrote for Windows95 (and NT) run at full speed on an
> Alpha?

Only if they are native alpha applications--which should be fairly
simple for a software co. to produce, at least in theory.

> MS-Office 95 and processor hungry games like Quake, Unreal and Command
> & Conquer 2. Only one of these games is designed for Windows 95 and NT
> (Unreal) so how would the others run thru the emulator?

You can run Quake on Alpha/Linux, but unless you use a VGA graphics
card, or care to program the onboard TGA, performance isn't all that
great.  The other programs are said to work with the FX!32 translator.
Much as I'd like to recommend the Alpha, I suspect that a pentium is the
better choice for you--even if I think the Multia is good value for
money at the current prices.

> What types of hardware are compatible with Alpha's.  I'm talking about
> IDE Drives, Sound cards, ISA Slots Memory and Modems.

Many alpha boxes come with ISA and PCI buses in them, the Multia only
has one PCI slot, but it does have onboard just-about-everything.

> Finally how does this Alpha running Windows NT compare to a Pentium
> Pro running NT?

I'd say an Alpha probalby will outshine P[56] at numerical work, but
then you'd need a higher-end processor than what you'll find in the
Multia. 

~kzm



