This reminds me of when we were working on the Denelcor HEP
computer and getting ready to write the C compiler.  The
HEP had 8, 16, 32, and 64 bit quantities (and this was back
in 1981).  Well, it's basic unit was 64, 32 was a partial
word for it.  So it was pretty clear we wanted to make 
int==long==64.  So what do we call the 32 bit things.
I voted for short longs (or long shorts) and others voted
for a new keyword "medium."  (We finally settled on _int32).

The HEP was amusing in that the partial word access was quaintly
encoded in the lower 3 bits of the address.  While the compiler
handled:

	int *x;
	short *y;
	x = &foo;
	y = x;

Doing something like this (which the BSD kernel we were porting
does all over the file system code, naughty, naughty)....

	union {
	     int * x;
	     short *y;
	} p;

	p.x = foo;
	woo = *p.y;

The assignment into woo would be of an "int" sized thing rather
than a short sized thing.

-Ron



