From: henning@i4get.enet.dec.com (John L. Henning)
Subject: HP Ushers In Unprecedented Hype, Shatters ?What? Record?
Date: 1996/09/18
Message-ID: <51q3p5$ujk@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>
X-Deja-AN: 181483205
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
newsgroups: comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.sys.dec,comp.benchmarks



                    HP Ushers in Unprecedented Hype,
                 Shatters Post-Season Record For Right
              Handed Batters Against Left Handed Pitching
                    In Day Games on Artificial Turf

                                   Or

         Anyone Can Win if the Category is Drawn Small Enough


This posting is in response to a September 16 HP Press release titled:

        HP Launches 64-Bit PA-8000 Enterprise Servers; Ushers in 
        Unprecedented Era of Computing Power, Price/Performance and 
        Internet Performance  
              http://www.hp.com:80/csopress/96sep16d.html

and a companion September 17 Aberdeen piece titled:

        HP 9000 Enterprise Server Performance Leadership Again: 
        The 64-bit PA-8000 Has Arrived
                http://www.aberdeen.com/secure/profiles/hp9000/hp9000.htm

These two documents are quoted below as "HP>" and "Ab>".

HP says it has:

HP> the world's fastest and most powerful RISC processor shipping
HP> today, the HP PA-8000(1). 

False.  The Alpha 21164 in the AlphaStation 500/500 is substantially
faster for SPECint95, and slightly faster for SPECfp95:

                     HP K460         AlphaStation 500/500
                     -------         --------------------
    SPECint95          11.8                 15.0
    SPECint_base95     10.8                 12.6
    SPECfp95           20.2                 20.4
    SPECfp_base95      18.3                 18.3

The AlphaStation results were submitted to SPEC during August, and are
expected to appear at their web site, http://www.specbench.org within
days.  I spoke to Digital Semiconductor Marketing today, who confirm
that Digital is shipping the 500 MHz Alpha 21164 now, both externally
and internally.  AlphaStation Product Management confirms that the
AlphaStation 500/500 (a workstation that uses the 500 MHz part) will
ship during September.  

HP says:

HP> The best of the new PA-8000-based family of servers is the HP 9000 
HP> K-Class, which recently shattered previous records for TPC-C benchmarks 
HP> in a midrange system, with 12,321 transactions-per-minute (tpmC) at 
HP> $187 per tpmC.(2) 

Shattered what?  Go visit the TPC web site, http://www.tpc.org, and
pull down the spreadsheet dated September 13, 1996.  Note the July,
1996 AlphaServer 8400 result of 14176.61 tpmC at $198 per tpmC - HP
hasn't "shattered" that record, HP offers 13.1% less performance with
only 5.5% better price/performance.  Nor for that matter has HP
"shattered" the April result from Sun for the Ultra Enterprise 4000,
11465.93 tpmC at $188 per tpmC - HP offers only 7.5% better
performance than Sun at 0.5% better price/performance.  

Maybe HP's statement could be somehow made true if the term "midrange"
were defined, but the press release offers no definition.  Let's see,
if we define midrange to mean "systems offering 10,000 to 15,000 tpmC
at a cost of under $200 per tpmC", then HP's performance comes in
second after the AlphaServer.  OK, suppose the category is "systems
offering 10,000 to 15,000 tpmC at a cost of less than $197 per tpmC". 
HP wins now, passing the Sun system by 7.5%.  A 7.5% win is a win, and
HP should be congratulated.  But a 7.5% win is not the "shattering" of
anything.  And the category had to be drawn very narrowly for HP to
eke out its win.

HP says:

HP> The HP 9000 D-Class servers also redefine entry-level performance, with 
HP> the industry's best RISC entry-level performance results of 5,822 tpmC 
HP> at $157 per tpmC.

Too bad HP hasn't seen fit to tell us exactly what category it
"redefines" for this result.  Perhaps the keyword in the sentence is
"RISC" - thus neatly excluding systems such as the July Digital
Prioris ZX 6166 result of 5740 tpmC at $117 per tpmC (1.4% less
performance than HP, 25.5% better price/performance). 

HP also demonstrates very careful category drawing in its discussion
of SPECweb results:

HP> HP 9000 enterprise servers demonstrated superior Internet performance
HP> with recent SPECweb benchmarks, which established the K-Class system as
HP> the fastest Internet processor in the computer industry with 801 SPECweb
HP> operations per second in a uniprocessor configuration. 

The K-Class system is NOT the fastest; according to the SPEC webpage
that honor currently belongs to the AlphaServer 2100 5/300, at 809
SPECweb96.  Digital has also submitted results for the AlphaServer
4100 5/400 of 974 SPECweb96, which will be posted soon by SPEC, and
which were announced by Digital on September 4 (see
http://www.digital.com/info/alphaserver/news/4000_8000_pr_996.html).

So how can HP possibly claim leadership here?  Well, if you read HP's 
sentence VERY carefully, you'll note a clause at the end of the
sentence "in a uniprocessor configuration".  True, Digital's results
are SMP results (4 CPUs on the 2100, 2 CPUs on the 4100).  But just
having the number of CPUs gives no information about which system may
be the better bargain.  HP used 2GB of memory to achieve their 1-cpu
result of 809 SPECweb96, vs. 1GB for the 2-cpu AlphaServer 4100. 
Which costs more, an add-in CPU or an add-in gigabyte?  HP's pricing
for an add-in GB in their May, 1996 TPC disclosure for the HP 9000
Model D360 was on the order of $76,000.  I don't know the exact
pricing of Digital CPUs, but I'm sure we would sell you at least 2
cpus for less than the cost of one HP gigabyte (see for example
http://www.digital.com/info/alphaserver/news/4000_8000_pr_996.html).

HP> HP believes they could achieve as many as 2,000 Web operations/s in 
HP> a four-way configuration.

It's easy to believe in whatever you like...what's been proven?

HP> The HP 9000 K460 offers unmatched midrange file-serving performance as
HP> measured by the SPEC LADDIS benchmark of 9,572 operations/s at a
HP> 19.9-millisecond response time.

What's the definition of "midrange" this time?  How does HP manage to
exclude Digital's result of 9965 operations/s at 25.3-milliseconds,
the Auspex result of 10084 ops at 11.3 ms, SGI's 10529 ops at 17.9ms,
and so forth? (All of these results are currently posted to the SPEC
website.)

When HP turns to application benchmarks, for once it cannot find a way
to construct a category that it can claim to be the best:

HP> In application benchmarks running SAP R/3 Release 3.0 D with Oracle 7.3
HP> database system, the HP 9000 Model K460 supported 1,400 Sales and
HP> Distribution benchmark users with an average dialog response time of 1.55
HP> seconds.(3) These performance leaps provide customers with a new era of
HP> choice in entry-level and midrange UNIX system servers to support growing
HP> application requirements.

HP is of course silent about Digital's result of 1700 SD benchmark
users with an average response time of 1.44 sec (reference press
release of March 12, 1996,
http://www.UNIX.digital./com/new/press7.html).

Later in HP's press release, HP returns to the topic of SPEC CPU95:

HP> HP's PA-8000 microprocessor has achieved SPEC95 ratings of 11.8 SPECint95
HP> and 20.2 SPECfp95. For compute-intensive tasks, the PA-8000 20.2 SPECfp95
HP> rating is more than 40 percent faster than the nearest competitor in its
HP> class. 

What class?  Left handed hitters in day games on artificial turf?  
You would have to draw the class pretty narrowly to be sure you
excluded all three of the Alpha results below:

                                SPECint95   SPECfp95
                                ---------   --------
        AlphaStation 500/500      15.0        20.4
        HP K460                   11.8        20.2
        AlphaServer 4100 5/400    12.1        17.2
        AlphaServer 8200 5/440    13.6        16.2

HP's SPECfp95 rating isn't 40% faster than any of these.   Note: the
first two Alpha results were submitted to SPEC during August and will
appear at the SPEC website soon.  Digital posted the 20.4 SPECfp on
its own web pages September 5th - see
http://www.alphastation.digital.com/products/a500/performance.html. 
The third Alpha result is already posted by SPEC.

HP> Advanced optimizing compilers and "intelligent execution" allow the
HP> PA-8000 to attain superscalar performance levels unmatched by other
HP> vendors.

Unmatched, maybe.  But not unbeaten.  And by the way, Digital's
compilers are state of the art, with improvements of up to 26% in
integer performance over the last year (reference SPEC web site
postings of performance for the AlphaStation 200 4/233 in July 1995
and July 1996).  

Returning to the topic of TPC, HP makes an incorrect statement
regarding Digital's results:

HP> HP today introduced four new servers in its K-Class family of
HP> enterprise servers -- Models K250, K260, K450 and the record-breaking
HP> K460, which has shattered previous audited midrange system
HP> transaction-per-minute benchmarks with 12,321 tpmC at $187 per tpmC.
HP> In the midrange-systems arena, the K460 offers the following:
HP> ...
HP>   * 65 percent better performance than Digital Equipment Corporation's 
HP>     4100 

Please reference the TPC web site mentioned above, results as posted
through September 13.  The AlphaServer 4100 provides 7985.15 tpmC at
$174 per tpmC using Oracle Rdb7, or 7598.63 tpmC at $152 per tpmC
using Sybase.  The K series has a clear win for performance on this
one, but NOT by 65%, it's either 54% or 62%, depending on which result
you compare to (resist temptation to insert sarcastic remark about
floating point arithmetic accuracy here).  But HP *loses* this
comparison on price/performance.    Or, one could compare the K460 to
Digital's AlphaServer 8400 result of 14176.61 tpmC at $198 per tpmC,
where the K series loses clearly on performance, with only a mild win
on price/performance.

HP continues its incorrect statements regarding Digital in the next
paragraph:

HP> With these levels of performance, HP's K-Class offers better performance
HP> than other vendors' high-end servers:
HP>...
HP>    * K-Class is more than 65 percent faster than Digital's high-end Model
HP>      8200.

First of all, the 8200 is not Digital's high-end server.  The 8400 is. 
Had HP compared to Digital's 8400, it would have lost on performance vs.
each of 
 - 8400/Sybase         (14176.61 tpmC @ $198 per tpmC)
 - 8400/Informix       (13646.17 tpmC @ $277 per tpmC)
 - 8400/Oracle Rdb     (14227.25 tpmC @ $269 per tpmC)
 - 8400/Oracle Cluster (30390.00 tpmC @ $305 per tpmC).
Secondly, it it should be noted that Digital has Withdrawn the 8200
results (as shown at the TPC web site).

In the department of naked chutzpah, HP pretends to be a leader in
64-bit UNIX, when it's really near the tail end of the pack:

HP> Leading the Way to 64-bit Computing
HP> 
HP> HP's robust, standards-based UNIX operating-system environment, HP-UX,
HP> is the industry's leading enterprise-class UNIX operating system. In
HP> February 1996, HP-UX became the first UNIX operating system to be
HP> certified compliant with the single UNIX system specification, X/Open
HP> UNIX 95. In its latest iteration, released on Aug. 1, HP-UX Version
HP> 10.20 includes new features such as support for the PA-8000
HP> microprocessor, 64-bit integer register calculation, an increase in
HP> file size from 4GB to 128GB.

Digital UNIX received X/Open(r) branding for UNIX 95 on January 26,
1996, the same date as HP-UX (reference
http://www.xopen.org/public/prods/company.htm). Digital UNIX has
supported 64-bit registers and file sizes greater than 4GB since it
first shipped in 1993.    HP's customers must be glad that HP's
"leadership" has them only 3 years behind Alpha!  Or maybe it's more
than 3 years:

HP> HP also announced plans to evolve to a full 64-bit version of HP-UX,
HP> with an expected release of mid-1997,

Thanks for admitting that you're not done.  Digital UNIX is 64 bits
from the ground up, and has been since 1993.

Meanwhile, the Aberdeen release is filled with TPC "fair use"
violations, for example:

Ab> HP estimates that the T600 will have a performance rating of over
Ab> 15,000 tpmC when it is delivered.

Ab> T600s will be used as SMP nodes to create the new EPS31 highly parallel
Ab> processing system to be delivered also in 1Q97. HP projects that a 4-node
Ab> EPS31 using 48 PA-8000 processors will be capable of delivering performance
Ab> of over 35,000 tpmC. 

The Transaction Processing Council Fair Use Policy clearly says that 
"It is unfair use [of TPC results] to use results, metrics, or
terminology which are not based upon official TPC results, but which
could be reasonably inferred to refer to the TPC or TPC workloads, or
to be comparable to TPC results."   (See http://www.tpc.org/fairuse.html)

Digital has protested this violation of TPC fair use policies.

One final note.  Everybody wants to win at something.  It's not
surprising that HP has tried to find categories to let it claim to be
the best, and Digital may have done so from time to time too (e.g. "best
single-node", "best single processor").  What has my dander up is that
HP has apparently (1) picked amazingly arbitrary categories, akin to 
gerrymandering;  and (2) neglected to clarify what the categories
*are*.  Perhaps TPC might want to consider adopting a fair use rule
that would at least address point (2).  Such a rule would create a
more level playing field, and compliance by all vendors (including HP
and  Digital) could easily be checked.

    /John Henning
     CSD Performance Group
     Digital Equipment Corporation
     henning@zko.dec.com




