>              Suddenly the HP K class performance is up there at the
>level of the 8400, with a processor that is so far as SPEC benchmarks
>are concerned actually slower in integer operations than the 350 & 400
>MHz Alpha. Moreover the Runaway bus on the K class offers less memory
>bandwidth than that of the bus of both the 4*00 and 8*00 AlphaServers.
>Latency isn't supposed to be much lower (unless I missed some HP
>announcement to that effect). So so far as comp.benchmarks readers are
>concerned the interesting question is *why* are the HP results so high
>when the components making them up do not appear to be (at least from
>what is available information now) do not appear to be so much higher
>(if at all) than the competition. 

HP has said that the bandwidth of the Runway bus is 960 mb/sec.  
Applying the usual ratio, one would expect 1/2 that in actual
McCalpin bandwidth, though it would be nice to see someone confirm
or deny that hypothesis and post the results to John McCalpin.  But
anyway if Runway does get on the order of 0.5GB/sec McCalpin bandwidth,
that puts it at about the same bandwidth as the AlphaServer 4100 and
about 1/2 the bandwidth of the AlphaServer 8400.

But the big problem for TPC-C (as detailed by Dick Sites a year or 
so ago) is not memory bandwidth; it's cache bandwidth for the 
i-stream.  The K460 has the virtue of a fast off-chip i-cache; 
the Alpha 21164 has a larger, but slower off-chip cache (as well as
fast on-chip caches).  So if a problem size fits into the K460's 
Icache, and doesn't fit into the 21164's on-chip caches, HP 
hits a "sweet spot".  Expect to see different scaling for TPC-D, 
AIM, and SPEC than for TPC-C.

-- 
         /John Henning
          CSD Performance Group
          Digital Equipment Corporation
