Subject: [Q] Can a VS200 boot over its AUI ethernet connector?
Date: 1996/09/19
Message-ID: <1996Sep19.090429.1@sldb3.slac.stanford.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 183966920
sender: news@unixhub.SLAC.Stanford.EDU
organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms


        Question: Has anyone ever successfully booted a VS2000 over its
    	--------  AUI Ethernet connection?

        The reason I ask is we've got  a  dozen  or so of these old boat
    anchors  that  still serve a purpose as EWS X-terminals.  For  years
    they've MOP loaded EWS from our cluster servers,  connected  to  our
    Thinwire  ethernet  cable plant, i.e, using the BNC ethernet port on
    the VS2000's.

        Now the networking people at my  site decided we should redo the
    whole  cable plant with 10baseT (aka, unshielded twisted pair, utp).
    Most of our existing VAXstations (and servers) have an AUI connector
    in addition to  a  BNC  (or  only),  and  only  our  test-bed  Alpha
    3000-400s  has  an  RJ-45 connection for UTP.  So the network people
    supplied us with a bunch  of AUI-to-UTP converters called "CentreCOM
    210TS"  "Twisted Pair Transceiver"  "IEEE 802.3 10 base T".    These
    seem to work great on all our VS3100's and VS4000's (various  models
    of  each).   Where there is a clearance problem, or where the 15-pin
    connector  has  "posts"  instead  slide-locks,  we've  added  a  12"
    pig-tail  that  simply has a male connector on one end with  screws,
    and a female connector on the other end with  a  slide-lock,  before
    going into the converter.  The pig-tails work fine on our 4000/VLC's
    for  example.   We use the pig-tails plus converters on the VS2000's
    because there is no clearance  for  the converter to attach directly
    (and no slide-lock).

        Having changed the entire  cluster  to the 10baseT calble plant,
    we  find  the VS2000's can no longer boot EWS (or anything else  for
    that matter).  Doing a ">>>b es" results in:

        >>>b es
        -ESA0

        %VMB-F-ERR, PC = 00000765
        %VMB-I-STS, R0 = 00000056
        ?06 HLT INST
            PC = 00000E64
        >>>

    If in place of  the  AUI-to-UTP  converter,  we reconnect one of the
    Thinwire segments directly to the BNC, or connect a short segment of
    Thinwire  to a Thinwire-to-UTP converter and plug the Thinwire  into
    the BNC, the VS2000 boots normally.

        Another interesting point.  The network  people put a sniffer on
    the  line  and watched as a sample VS2000 tried to boot, first  over
    its BNC, and then over its AUI.  When booting over the BNC, it sends
    out a MOP/DL packet about once every 6 seconds, waiting  in  between
    for a response from a boot host.  The server nodes typically respond
    after  about  6  seconds  to  the first MOP/DL request packet.  When
    trying to boot over the AUI,  the VS2000 sends 48 consecutive MOP/DL
    packets  with  no wait in between, waits about 6 seconds,  and  then
    declares an error are returns to the >>> prompt.

        So the question is why is  there  a  difference in the timing of
    the  MOP/DL  packets sent over the BNC versus the AUI port?   Anyone
    know of an inherent restriction using the AUI port?  Would  presence
    or  lack or a "heartbeat" have anything to do with it?  (This is old
    equipment!)  Other ideas?

        -Ken
