Well, these numbers are not yet publically available on the SPEC
Web site.  The best numbers for Alpha today are the 8400/440 numbers
at 
     SPECint95          13.6
     SPECfp95           16.2

So yes an 8400/440 beats a K460 on the int side, but is still
lacking on fp.

I would not call SPEC a great measure of how well a processor
will act under a wider range of applications (like database and
fileserver).

>HP says:
>
>HP> The best of the new PA-8000-based family of servers is the HP 9000 
>HP> K-Class, which recently shattered previous records for TPC-C benchmarks 
>HP> in a midrange system, with 12,321 transactions-per-minute (tpmC) at 
>HP> $187 per tpmC.(2) 
>
>Shattered what?  Go visit the TPC web site, http://www.tpc.org, and
>pull down the spreadsheet dated September 13, 1996.  Note the July,
>1996 AlphaServer 8400 result of 14176.61 tpmC at $198 per tpmC - HP
>hasn't "shattered" that record, HP offers 13.1% less performance with
>only 5.5% better price/performance.  Nor for that matter has HP
>"shattered" the April result from Sun for the Ultra Enterprise 4000,
>11465.93 tpmC at $188 per tpmC - HP offers only 7.5% better
>performance than Sun at 0.5% better price/performance.  

But if you take a closer look at the boxes you will see quite a
difference in performance prcoessor for processor:

Machine   tpmC    #processors   MHz    Memory used  Datbase
--------------------------------------------------------
DEC 8400  14,176.61   10        350        6GB       Sybase V11
SUN 5000  11,465.93   12        167        5GB       Sybase V11
HP K460   12,321.85    4        180        3GB       Sybase V11

So with 1/2 the memory and 2.5 times fewer processors than DEC
HP has gotten to within 13% of DEC's number!  This is using HP's
midrange vs DEC's high-end.

Just dividing the tpmC by # of processors gives the following
breakdown:

Machine    tpmC/processor
--------------------------
K460         3080
DEC 8400     1418 
Sun 5000      955

Now due to scaling issues a real uniprocessor could get much
higher than these values, but it does show that the K460 really
does "shatter" the competition.

The reason the K460 is not much cheaper in terms of $/tpmC is
due to mainly pricing games played by DEC and Sun.  In addition,
the HP disclosure uses ALL HA disk arrays (running RAID5 and
RAID1) whereas the DEC and Sun use only arrays for the log device.
The HP disclosure represents a "real word configuration".  No 
real customer would run in a JBOD configuration with 770GB of
disk storage!

If you just compare the price of the K460 vs a DEC 8400 then
the real delta becomes obvious( take away all  of the pricing games).

>
>Maybe HP's statement could be somehow made true if the term "midrange"
>were defined, but the press release offers no definition.  Let's see,
>if we define midrange to mean "systems offering 10,000 to 15,000 tpmC
>at a cost of under $200 per tpmC", then HP's performance comes in
>second after the AlphaServer.  OK, suppose the category is "systems
>offering 10,000 to 15,000 tpmC at a cost of less than $197 per tpmC". 
>HP wins now, passing the Sun system by 7.5%.  A 7.5% win is a win, and
>HP should be congratulated.  But a 7.5% win is not the "shattering" of
>anything.  And the category had to be drawn very narrowly for HP to
>eke out its win.
>
Again you need to look at the system cost and not $/tpmC.  The DEC
8400 is a high-end box.  With 12 processors and 5GB of memory, the
Sun would also be considered a high-end box (but probably at the
low-end of the high-end).  The K460 is a mid-range box in the
same class as a DEC 4100 5/400 (which it soundly beats in the TPC-C
tests -- DEC 4100 5/400 only gets 7598.63 with Sybase V11 -- or
1900 tpmC/processor)
   
>>HP also demonstrates very careful category drawing in its discussion
>of SPECweb results:
>
>HP> HP 9000 enterprise servers demonstrated superior Internet performance
>HP> with recent SPECweb benchmarks, which established the K-Class system as
>HP> the fastest Internet processor in the computer industry with 801 SPECweb
