sell, or give away, the equipment either.

Yes, license a computer system, with an infinite-duration license, for
300,000 dollars, use it for a two years and be sure that you dispose
of it properly (smash it to bits, in this case - yes, that's
sickening, I agree) when you're finished with it.  Don't dare try to
give it to someone, or sell it to someone, in working condition.
Don't dare try to recoup part of your investment, as it would be
illegal for you to do so, never mind that you could have used it
indefinitely and DEC would have made no more money off of you.

Now do you see how ridiculous this software licensing scheme of DEC's
is, particularly when one imagins how it would be if taken just a bit
further?

>DEC did not sell VMS to the previous owner of that machine. They sold 
>a license to use VMS. There were terms relating to transferability to 
>which the original buyer agreed.

Well, the buyer didn't have much choice, did he?  One needs an OS to
run on these machines, and, untill recently, there were no free
versions of UNIX to run on them, so the average buyer was generally
stuck with having to purchase that license from DEC in order to use
his equipment.  

>>them, from DEC.  When someone stops using one of these machines and
>>gives to someone else to use, with the OS on it, DEC loses no more
>>money than they would have lost than if the original purchaser or
>>licensee kept using the machine.  There's no element of time involved
>
>Yes of course they do. They lose the second sale. A 100% loss.

Humbug!  In many cases, the old equipment goes to someone, or some
organization, who can't afford to purchase a new machine, or
even a new OS license, from DEC in the first place.  Hence DEC
loses nothing.  Go back and give this some thought. 

>>in the licenses, and no reoccuring yearly charges, hence, requiring
>>someone to transfer a license, at a cost, basically amounts to
>>something similar to extortion.
>
>That argument is irrelevant. VMS is theirs to do with as they please. 
>They own it.

No, it is not irrelevant.  Apparently you haven't bothered to give all
of this some good, deep, thought.

>Extortion? C'mon.

Yes, extortion - with a capital E. 

