Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:

>Once upon a time, it was impossible to even *transfer* a VMS license,
>even if you paid a fee.  What DEC wanted to do is eliminate the
>used equipment market - 

Very interesting.  It appears that they're still trying to eliminate
part of it, for when the used equipment drops below a certain value,
some people aren't going to want to pay more to transfer an OS license
than the hardware is worth.  So, in a way, it appears that DEC is
still involved, to some extent, in such a scheme.  

>they reasoned that each used machine sold
>was a lost sale of new equipment.  

That's rather senseless thinking being displayed by DEC.  After all,
not everyone who uses used equipment is going to purchase new
equipment.  They'll either use equipment from another manufacturer,
use the equipment illegally or just do without computer equipment.

On the other hand, those who are using the used equipment may decide
to purchase new DEC equipment when they can afford to do so, if not
discouraged from using DEC's older equipment.  DEC is just hurting DEC
by taking this approach to things.

>So by pretty much
>banning license transfers altogether, they thought they would sell
>more new hardware.  Of course, what they did instead was piss off
>all their users and the resellers.  

Indeed.

>After threats of legal action
>(I believe with the words "anti-trust" in them) DEC finally put
>together a system where the used equipment resellers could transfer
>"regular" licenses.  Find issues of _Digital Review_ from the
>early 80's for the details.

Very interesting.

>>  There's no element of time involved
>>in the licenses, and no reoccuring yearly charges, hence, requiring
>>someone to transfer a license, at a cost, basically amounts to
>>something similar to extortion.
>
>No, they always own the software.  

I understand that, but still think that this amounts to extortion, and
don't believe that their practices amount to fair trade.  

Of course, DEC doesn't own the media, and can't really control what
happens with it.  If someone happens to be driving down the road, and
a few disk packs, tapes or floppies fall out of their car while on the
way to the dump, or to the Ye Old Bulk Media Erasing and Bat Cave
Supplies Shop, and, a few hours later someone walks by and picks them
up from the gutter, where they fell next to a dumpster, appearing to
have been discarded, and uses the OS on them, and doesn't know about
the licensing...

Furthermore, if someone does own magnetic media with a DEC OS on it,
and has no time, or no longer has the necessary equipment, to erase
it, and wants to sell the magnetic media, they have the legal right to
do so, as long as they're not selling it for its contents.  Now then,
it's quite possibly that the purchaser happens to have some DEC
equipment with no OS, and knows nothing about how DEC OS software is
licensed.  The person just thinks - oh goodie!  I can now use this
computer equipment that I found in a dumpster!  Who's legally at
fault?  Anyone?

>You've just paid them the license fee
>to use it.  And there are VMS and product licenses that expire - CSLUG
>licenses are the example I run across most often.

Are expiring licenses something more recent, or is their use
increasing?   Does the software stop working at the end of the
licensing period? 

