Patrick Gainer wrote:
[snip]
> But with respect to 64 bit systems, I think there is little doubt that
> 64 bit systems combined with falling memory prices allow significant
> performance advantages for large RDBMS installations, regardless of the
> RDBMS vendor (assuming that RDBMS vendor has a 64 bit product).
> 

Every one of the major database vendors have 64bit capable database
today (not in any specific order: Oracle, Informix, Sybase, Sofware AG,
Progress, CA, ... ). In most cases even porting to 64bits does not take
any special efforts:

	- pointers are automagically 64bits (if not truncated with
	  compiler option aka. forced to fit 32bits)
	- underlying operating system services and Run Time Livbraries
	  use 64bit pointer _AND_ integer arithmetics (for example
	  fast string routines)

So, even "traditional" applications can utilize 64bitness in real
life with minimal effort from application programmers point of view.
And in some cases, rewriting some algorithms can bring even huge
advantage (for example encryption technologies), and in many cases
compilers know how to improve your algorithms (for example multimedia).

Every application on Digital UNIX (on Alpha) uses 64bit features 
out of the box :-) Many RDBMS related operations (indexing/string
comparing/string and data manipulation/sorting) use these services
without even noting it if they use operating system or library
primitives.


> Pat

