That's what I figured.  Hence, this leads to the scrapping of older,
but still useful, equipment - still gets some used equipment out of
the market; just what DEC wants.  As the hardware's value drops below
the cost of the licenses, a reseller can't sell a customer a working
system for less than the $300 cost of the license transfer.  As a
result, some older equipment becomes, according to some, "obsolete",
before it should, and ends up being sold for scrap instead of as a
working gsystem.  Of course, I don't believe that any system is is
ever truly obsolete.  I'm sure that this $300 transfer fee is used by
DEC for this purpose.

It seems that $300 is a rather steep price to charge just to shuffle a
few papers around.  Now, I still don't agree with this business of the
way DEC licenses their OS software, but I could see perhaps $5 or so
being a more reasonable fee.  It should only take a clerical employee
about a minute to pop an application in the mail to someone, postage
shouldn't cost very much, then another minute or so to file the
application away in a file cabinet when it arrives.  

Let's see - 5 minutes total processing time per application at $5 each
means that 12 applications can be processed by one clerk in one hour.
That comes to $60 for DEC.  Now then, the clerk certainly makes far
less than that, probably about $10 or less per hour.  Postage would be
no more than 32 cents.  After a few hundred applications, the cost of
the file cabinet and clerk's desk, etc. are paid for and DEC is making
a profit.  Now then, let's suppose that a photocopy of the application
has to be made and filed away as well; DEC would still make a profit.

