Right, which is why you need to be able to separate the accurate
information about the future from the propaganda.  PG is right: if TPC
(or SPEC, for that matter) required all official ratings to be on
shipping systems, we could compare apples to apples in an easy fashion.
Since all manufactures produce faster machines each year there needs
to be a CONCRETE date associated with each system benchmarked, such as
WHEN IT FIRST STARTED SHIPPING to the public.  If a customer wants to
wonder about what's in store for the future I personally think he/she
should only go on past performance (extend the speed vs. shipping date
plot . . .).  But if he/she wants to listen to propaganda, note that
companies could always say, "... is estimated to yield xxx on the TPC
benchmark ..."  In fact, most manufacturers already do this today
anyways (the xyz processor is estimated at xxx SPECFP95 and is
scheduled to sample in 1997 . . . ).  Therefore, there is no harm at
all in forcing the OFFICIAL TPC entries to be from shipping systems.

So in summary, companies can and will continue to claim what they want
about un-shipping items.  But TPC (and SPEC, for that matter) should
separate the fact from fiction.  They can do this by requiring that
all benchmarks are only for shipping systems.  If someone really wants
to plan ahead they can read a company's web page under the "Latest TPC
Results from our Lab about our Superduperfast new Microprocessor we
are Trying to Make" section.  Finally, people (John D.?) who make
tables of TPC (or SPEC) scores should be kind enough to include the
complete hardware shipping (not reporting) date as a column.

