|> 	V3.2x...

I really don't want to sound like a broken record on this,
but, I feel the point keeps being lost that the *system*
availability includes the hardware, the OS, and the 
database software.  I know from personal experience that
it is one thing to get a prototype of this working long
enough to run a benchmark, and another thing to have
a production system using the production software,
with the correct combination of hardware, OS, including
the inevitable patches/options/whatever necessary to make
it work with the database, AND, the corresponding RDBMS,
AND, have the whole thing stay up, not cause data corruption,
be able to have production stuff like backups, error recovery,
etc.  Of course, maybe this has all gotten trivially easy 
very recently ;-) , but, it wasn't before that.  Sure,
we may be only talking about a small software "component".
But, anyone who has struggled with any of this knows 
what that might mean.

That is why I tend to support the previously made point that 
to be fair, *everything* would have to be shipping, production,
components, in a currently available and supported configuration.


|> [snip]
|> > Unless Sun comes out with a new-and-improved "UltraSparc 3",
|> > which, *as far as I know*, they might.  Or, maybe SGI/MIPS
|> > will get back on top.  Or, HP.  Or, IBM, Or, x86.  Or, ...
|> [snip]
|> 
|> 	We've never even thought that Sun would be as sitting
|> 	duck here ... we're anxious to see UltraSPARC's next
|> 	generation :-)
|> 
|> 	Reason why availability dates are in future in many cases
|> 	is that we must be sure that we can deliver our promises
|> 	and in many cases there is safety margin if tested system
|> 	has used un-announced technology. For example latest TPC-C
|> 	and TPC-D test results are based on techology you can
|> 	already buy today if you have the money and need for such
|> 	system!

They are based (in this case - they are not always) based on
hardware and operating systems that are shipping, I take it?
That's good wrt the HW performance comparison with Sun, but it
still doesn't address the question of when the complete system
including the RDBMS will be running with the same components
as the benchmark system.

I'm not, actually, picking on DEC particularly about this.
It is just a general remark that, as was pointed out previously,
a weakness of the TPC benchmarks is that there is no guarantee
that the complete hardware/os/software system will be operational
in the stated configuration with the stated performance at the
stated future date.


