Subject: Help: NFS (?) hangs net when compiling, SunOS 4.1.4/Digital Unix 4.0
Date: 1996/09/13
Message-ID: <51cdu0$7fc@camelot.ccs.neu.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 180455456
organization: College of Computer Science, Northeastern University.
newsgroups: comp.sys.sun.admin,comp.sys.dec


Hi.  We've been consistently able to completely halt our net by running
a particular configure script (i.e., a GNU ./configure shell script) on
an Alpha running Digital Unix 4.0, where the compilation directory is
NFS mounted from a Sun running (sigh) SunOS 4.1.3.  (Of course, the
Alpha can do NFS 3 or 2, but the Sun can only do NFS 2.)

The script is actually running gcc at the time the net gets trashed.

Sniffing the net when things go down shows huge amounts of NFS traffic
between those two machines.  At that point the net is unusable; some
machines are completely unresponsive, and some will echo characters
typed only after several minutes (~5-10, I think).

My suspicion is that this may be some sort of compatibility problem
between the versions of NFS on the two machines, triggered by a
particular pattern of NFS activity.

The problem occurs with any of our Digital Unix 4.0 machines, but not
3.0.  (However, the 3.0 machines we've tested, although on the same
subnet, are using different wiring.  Also, they'd be running a different 
version of the compiler.)

The Alphas are all on a token-ring network hanging off a Digital
GigaSwitch.  One of them is the gateway to the rest of our net.  The
Sun involved is not on the same subnet as the gateway machine, so the
NFS traffic is going through the GigaSwitch, the Alpha acting as
router, and our Cisco router to get to the NFS server.

There's NFS service from some other machines infolved as well, but we
don't think that was the problem, based on sniffing the net.

Has anybody seen anything like this?  Is this a known problem?  Are
there any NFS tuning parameters we can set either on the Sun or the
Alpha that might make a difference?  Or are we barking up the wrong
tree altogether?

Responses to me, please; I'll summarize for the groups.

Thanks,

Jay Sekora
<jay@ccs.neu.edu>

PS - News flash: we've gotten the same thing to happen with another
./configure script for another package.  It happens when trying to
compile a C file consisting of only "int main() { exit (0); }".
Moreover compiling the same source file with Digital's C compiler
produces a very similar result (but hangs a little earlier, leaving
foo.o instead of a.out).



