>Gordon Zaft <zaft@news.arizona.edu> wrote:
>>R. D. Davis (rdd@access1.digex.net) wrote:
>>: Speaking of such, does anyone have any information on "patching" a
>>: single-user, or small number of users, version of VMS to work with a
>>: larger number of users?  
>>	It's called 'buying more licenses from DEC'.  Any other means
>>would be highly illegal and not likely to be discussed in a reputable
>>newsgroup.
>Ilegal?  Nonsense!  If I want to modify some software in my posession
>in order to make it more useful, I'll modify it; doing so is merely
>logical.

Not that simple.  "Posessing" the software is not the same as
being licensed to use it.  Unless you have the magic pieces of
paper from DEC saying that you have the license, you don't have
the license.  Even if you have the original distribution kit,
this does not imply that you have any license to use it - the
distribution and the license are two mostly orthogonal things.
(I say "mostly" because sometimes DEC will insist that you have
a license before selling you a distribution - but sometimes they're
a bit more lax.)

Assuming you have the proper licenses for the software but don't
want all the hassles associated with the License Management Facility,
there are patches that you can apply to VMS such that you don't
have to go through the troublesome procedure of PAK's.  I've also
used such patches to help system managers who do have a license for
a product, but the company selling the product was unable to
properly supply a PAK for the particular product.  This particular
patch causes all checks for license PAK's to automatically succeed.
Interestingly enough, some non-DEC products check for "dummy"
licenses which are supposed to fail, and if these pass, it assumes
that you've screwed with the system and will refuse to start up.
There are ways of dealing with this case, too: in particular,
there's a different patch to LMF that will let you create your
own PAK's.

The LMF-disabling patch is one that a famous character once wore on
a T-shirt to a DECUS convention.  DECUS trivia question of
the week: *who* was that character?

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



