mash@mash.engr.sgi.com (John R. Mashey) wrote:

>It ought to be plausible that anybody motivated to
>do so, who also owns their CPU technology, might choose to build a
>microprocessor with more memory bandwidth (better do this, no matter
>what else you're doing), and vector-style capabilities.
>After all, the biggest difference between scalable DSMs and vector
>systems, over the long term, is in the kind of memory system you
>choose to build...

More memory bandwidth, vector-style capabilities.  Hmm.  Sound like
the R8000?  That one didn't fly very well with your graphics system
customers, too much emphasis on floating point for them.  Or are you
suggesting that SGI will introduce long sequential arithmetic
operations into the processor instruction set? VLIW, perhaps?  So far
it seems that in a competition for limited die real estate, floating
point loses at SGI.  This may have changed with the purchase of CRAY
(I hope so) in that there _may_ be a continuing plan to dominate in
the floating point market.

I concur with what you say about memory.  It is the bandwidth, stupid.
The R10K is a fine processor and SGI machines have had a secondary
memory bandwidth advantage over the competition for a long time.
Times started changing with the IBM 590.

In 1989, SGI, DEC, IBM, HP and SUN were all poor performers on our
bandwidth testing for CFD applications.  When compared to the Stellar,
Ardent, Convex and Cray machines of the time the difference in memory
bandwidth was stark.

SGI was the first to "figure it out" in about 1990-91 with some new
software and hardware for the 4D-80GT and 4D-110 machines (early R3000
or R2000?).   The problem was TLB thrashing and after SGI fixed it,
we started buying a lot of SGI machines for CFD and general
engineering computing.  The next company  to "figure it out" was IBM
with the 590.  DEC followed suit after a couple of years.

CPU speeds continue to advance at 2X/year, but memory speed and
technology moves in fits and starts.  Massive interleave and SSRAM are
expensive and special purpose, but they work well for CFD and like
problems (witness the SX-4).  Will we see a competitive but cheaper
technology appear in the arena soon?  If so, what will it look like?
0.11 micron process?  Will we continue to see the memory hierarchy
deepen with NUMA DSM and the continuance of cache?  It doesn't make
the job of optimal programming any easier (nested cache blocking is
not my idea of straightforward).


