Hi Tim!
This brings up a point in *my* little mind. What exactly *is*
the device protecting all their software? Is it a copyright?
If it's a copyright, then why isn't the "license" following the
distribution media? What good is a license that has no
underlying software? (SHEESH, the LAWYERS!). This "artificial"
contract pronouncement only has meaning when I apply for updates
or support from DEC. It doesn't make sense that they can
prohibit me from using a piece of software on their own machines
with their own distribution media; even though I have not
"applied" for permission to use it...no one applies for permission
to "own" or "possess" a book. No. The copyright follows the
distribution media, in this case. No other copies were made.

Tell me how the copyright laws can go any further?

I believe that it is a simple contract arrangement for service
and support in this "licence" agreement put forth by DEC. I just
simply don't see how they can proceed in any legal way against
me for possessing and using their original distribution media.
The original contract only binds the original purchaser. It
cannot bind me, except under laws (copyright) that may apply to
me. If I do not make copies and sell them, how am I violating
*any* law? If I *give* away, or *sell* the original media, then
how am I violating *any* law? If DEC causes the original
purchaser to sign a contract, and then that person(s) give
away the software to me, then DEC's problem is with the original
purchaser, not me. I cannot see that they have any legal recourse
against me...
