Time for a little history lesson:

Once upon a time, it was impossible to even *transfer* a VMS license,
even if you paid a fee.  What DEC wanted to do is eliminate the
used equipment market - they reasoned that each used machine sold
was a lost sale of new equipment.  So by pretty much
banning license transfers altogether, they thought they would sell
more new hardware.  Of course, what they did instead was piss off
all their users and the resellers.  After threats of legal action
(I believe with the words "anti-trust" in them) DEC finally put
together a system where the used equipment resellers could transfer
"regular" licenses.  Find issues of _Digital Review_ from the
early 80's for the details.

>  There's no element of time involved
>in the licenses, and no reoccuring yearly charges, hence, requiring
>someone to transfer a license, at a cost, basically amounts to
>something similar to extortion.

No, they always own the software.  You've just paid them the license fee
to use it.  And there are VMS and product licenses that expire - CSLUG
licenses are the example I run across most often.

