The fact is, owning a book and owning software is much the same.  You
don't own a novel, you own a printed copy of it.  You buy the book and
pay the license at the same time.  you are licensed to subject the book
to 'reasonable use'.  That is you can read it.  You can loan it to a friend.
You can't make a copy of it, and give that to a friend.  You can give
it away.  You can even legally make a complete copy of the book and store
the original for safekeeping, but if you give or sell the original away,
you must either give the copies at the same time, or destroy them.  O/S's
are much the same.  you are generally licensed to use the software on
the system you bought it with, and making a copy from disk or tape to
hard drive is not a violation.  If you have two exact same machines, and
you use the exact same O/S revision, and buy a single machine upgrade, you
can legally use it on only one machine.  A lot of the O/S's have a detailed
license/copyright statement on the container in which they come.

