There you go: the history lesson *proves* my point...that DEC
will extend themselves way out into *anti-trust* territory for
heaven's sake. Anti-trust is a criminal action! Seems to me
that locking out reselling scared the bejesus out of them.
All they did, by creating a "tax" on transfer was nullify the
action; not admit they were wrong in the first place.
Sort of like the government, any government, does. Tax
the bejesus out of people until anti-government types start
springing up like wildflowers, then back off on the tax a little
to nullify the movement.

What DEC did was render the user's equipment, regardless
of length of time of ownership, worthless: thereby stealing
from him. Did they offer to buy back the equipment on
a reasonable pro-rated basis? Probably not. Pretty much a type of
stealing to me.

What we have here is a classic clash in rights VERY similar
to one experienced when one buys a mobile home:
1. The property underneath the mobile home is owned by
the landowner.
2. The mobile home is owned by the purchaser.
3. Moving the mobile home usually destroys and/or renders
the mobile home worthless.
4. Through #3 above, the land owner gains indirect control
and ability to disrupt and or/render worthless the mobile
home owner's right to own it free of interference.
What happens is government steps in after recognizing that
no restraint on a mobilehome property owner leads to
property damage and/or loss to the mobile home owner, and
controls him; the government (here) makes it VERY difficult for him to
evict the mobile home owner. (It is a VERY lopsided control
proposition that the landowner has...
the land owner loses nothing in the eviction process, in effect
using the probable loss of value as a control)

The point is that DEC is in a position to cause property loss
or loss of value by its mere ability to control the license in
such a restrictive manner. By adding a "tax" in an effort to
keep that control, I STILL believe they are wrong because
they cause me to give up property (money) to sell property
when they would not have gained a thing without the transfer...
(this is seemingly a unique property of government: the ability
to add a transfer tax)...
the operating system, once attached to the machine, should
transfer unrestricted with the machine, once sold IMHO, because
the machine becomes worthless without it.

