In article <51ah9r$o1a@access1.digex.net>,
R. D. Davis <rdd@access1.digex.net> wrote:
>After carefully checking out my MicroVAX II (testing the PSU with only
>the M7553 in the QBus, cleaning out any dust, checking cables, making
>sure that no loose bits of metal were loose in it, etc.), I placed the
>CPU and memory boards back into the system, reassembled everything and
>powered the system up.
>
>The LED "countdown" display on the back of the chasis didn't light up
>at all, and a terminal connected to the console port doesn't display
>anything.  When powered up, the four LEDs on the back of the CPU board
>display the following pattern, where x=off and o=on:
>    
>                                     o
>                                     x
>                                     x
>                                     o
>

This is the code for "6", indicating "running address tests on RAM
memory".  The amount of time it spends in this mode is roughly
proportional to how much memory you have (with the proportionality
constant depending somewhat on whether it's DEC memory or 3rd-party
memory).

>After a few seconds, the pattern changes to:
>
>                                     o
>                                     x
>                                     o
>                                     o
>
>Now then, with just these three boards, CPU (M7606EF), memory
>(M7608BC) and the console interface (M7553) in the system, is this the
>correct behavior for this system?

I think so.  That last pattern is for "2" (LSB is on top), indicating
that it's searching for the boot device.  It won't ever find a boot
device until you put one in :-).  Depending on how the switches
on the console panel are set, it may be prompting the operator for
the boot device.

>  I was expecting a countdown on the
>LED and on the console, followed by the ">>>" prompt.

Is the M7553 a VCB01 or a VCB02, by any chance?  If so, the ">>>"
prompt is in video RAM and being sent to your not-hooked-up
VR290 monitor.  I have to admit that my memory is failing me
as to what a M7553 is at the moment.  But if it's a video display
card, the firmware on the CPU will detect it and send the output
there instead of to the console terminal you've attached.

>As to QBus termination, does the backplane in the MicroVAX contain the
>necessary termination, or does a terminator board need to be used, or
>does the M7553 provide the necessary termination for the bus?

The backplane almost certainly contains the termination needed.  Is this
a BA23 or a BA123 box?  There were a few odd variants with respect
to termination, but more than likely this isn't your problem.

You make it sound like the M7553 is in the last slot on the bus - is this
the RQDX3 distribution card by any chance?  Sorry, I've repressed
nearly all memories of VCB's and RQDX's over the past couple of years!

>Before powering up the system, I tested the rechargeable battery pack
>on the back of the back panel (batteries for the real-time clock?),
>and measured 0.5V.  After the system had been powered up for a few
>minutes, I disconnected the battery connector and measured the
>voltages again: 3.1V - still slightly lower than the 3.6V rating on
>the battery pack.  Could this be causing the problem?

No.  Eventually it'll charge up to 3.6V, most likely.  This is used
to store settings when the AC is off and run the TOY clock when
AC is off.  If it won't hold a charge, you can replace it with
any set of 3 nicad cells that'll physically fit.  They're easy to
find these days - they come already prepackaged as packs for
cordless phone handsets.

>Could the fact that there is no disk drive controller in the system be
>causing this problem?  I wouldn't think so, but still thought that it
>might be a good idea to ask.

That's probably why it's hanging at "2" - it's trying to autoboot from
a device, but you have no devices!

The serial terminal port is the 10-pin connector on the CPU board.
This runs to the cab kit, which has a couple of switches and a DB
connector for the terminal.  Is this where the terminal is plugged
into?  You say the LED's on the cab kit don't light up - is it
possible that you've plugged the cab kit into the M7553 instead
of the CPU?

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



