 Ok, here they go:

   VR299:

   Horizontal rate timing:

        Frequency    		:   54.054 Khz
        Front porch  		:   159 ns
        Sync pulse   		:   1,850 us
        Back porch   		:   1,680 us
        Blanking interval	:   3.69 us maximum
        Active video 		:   14.8 us
        Horizontal period	:   18.5 us

   Vertical rate timing:

	Frequency    		:   60 Hz
        Front porch  		:   0 horizontal lines
        Sync pulse   		:   3 lines
        Blanking interval  	:   37 lines
        Active video 		:   16 ms
        Vertical period		:   16.67 ms

   Video Rate(bandwidth):

        Pixel frequency         :   70 Mhz
        Pixel period            :   14.45 ns (nominal)    

   Sync on green
   Red/Blue/Green : 0.7 Vp 75 ohm
   Resolution : 1024x864

   These specs came from the VR299 Manual.
   NO wARRANTY ( But you already knew that , didn't you ? )
   I have 6 VR299 and I don't like them. Too much glare, low refresh.
   The VRT19 or the VR320, now these are good monitors...

   It has 3 BNC on the back, it sends the sync signals on the green
   color ( composite green). It is a fixed frequency monitor, so it
   only syncs at a special resolution.
   To use this monitor in a normal PC you need a special video card 
   or a small circuit that 'couples' the H and V sync signals to the
   green color signal. You can find such a circuit on the pc hardware
   video faq, among other places. There are a couple of then around...
   The special card is expensive but gives almost all the resolutions
   you need, but with the circuit you can only sync up one or two
   high-res modes . And your video card must supply the right vertical
   and horizontal timings ( they are NOT typical PC monitor values ).
   Linux ( well, XFree ... ) handles that job for you, but for Dos or
   Windows you must tweak the VGA board to do it. That depends now on
   your video card. Very few ship with drivers or programs to let you
   do that.
   Hope this helps, 
 

