Fred Kleinsorge wrote:
>
>   Because the amount of sparse and dense address space were not the same on the
>   AlphaServers, the hardware group chose to not overlap the address spaces.  The
>

  I'd like to modify the above.  It is not as clear cut as AlphaStations versus AlphaServers,
  as time has gone on the decision has been made on a platform basis.  So for instance, the
  AlphaServer 4100 does overlap the address spaces.

  I think the answer to the specific question was to change the PCI config space value to
  indicate prefetchable memory - and you will get dense space.

  If you want to create multiple mappings, you should use the O/S calls to map the address
  space with the proper granularity and then make sure and check the status from the call
  to make sure that it worked!  That is, do not assume that any address can be mapped in
  both of the spaces.  This is what the low-end servers on OpenVMS do - they attempt to map
  sparse and dense, but understand that they may need to punt on dense access to the
  frame buffer - based on the return from O/S call (in OpenVMS it's IOC$MAP_IO) that
  creates the virtual mapping.



