references: <1996Sep5.113209.946@flying-disk.com> <50qvup$kl8@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca> <50s59i$3sj@access4.digex.net> <50sapo$p9i@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca>
organization: Personal Opinions Only
keywords: ST-506 drives, high-capacity disk packs, portable clean rooms
reply-to: lederman@eisner.decus.org.disable.automated.mail
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



I would certainly agree that a lot of what was part of PM no
longer is required.  There are a few things I'd still do if
there were qualified people around who really knew how to do
it.

If you have any 'openings' in your system case, such as a
tape drive or floppy drive, then vaccuming the dust out
occasionally may well be a good idea.  If you are using
tape for backups, then cleaning the drive may also be a
good idea: but check with the drive manual first.

Depending on what kind of system and power supply you have
it may also be a good idea to check the voltages occasionally:
I've seen systems become erratic because the supply voltage
was a little low.  But I would only let someone who was really
trained do this.

If you don't have well trained personnel, and you don't have
any obvious dust paths into a case (such as a tape drive),
then it may well be better to leave the system alone.  Opening
it up and potentially disturbing cables or touching the wrong
thing may be worse than leaving a little dust in.

I would also agree that if you're running on really old hardware,
you should at least make backups and put a plan in place to replace
it with something newer.  You might want to wait until the system
fails (if down time isn't too critical), or you may want to replace
really old disks before they fail (if down time is more critical).

The way stuff is built today, there really isn't much to adjust or
clean.  It's usually better to run it for it's  predicted life, and
then think of replacement: and send the old stuff to a broker if
it's still working, or a parts house that can strip it, or at the
very least to a recycling company.

