|> 	you got 9/30/96 at.

The wrap-up summary actually lists 8/15/96.  Sorry about that.  
The 9/30/96 date was for the Ultra Enterprise 4000 and 5000.
[I'm not sure where you got 8/12/96, but the difference is small,
"even to me" ;-) ]

In any case, the Sun Ultra Enterprise 6000 should be 
shipping by now, as should the IBM and Tandem systems.  
Any comments out there on whether or not these dates 
have been (approximately) met?

As I said before, with performance increasing generally 
at a fast rate historically, actual shipping dates make
a big difference in how you compare these systems.  Some
of the benchmarks over the last couple of years have shown 
Alpha-based systems in the lead, SPARC-based systems
in the lead, MIPS-based systems in the lead, x86-based
systems in the lead.  But, *when* were these systems
actually in the hands of customers?  That is why the 
actual availability date is so important.

|> > |> 	Just goes to show how truly under-powered the UltraSparc is compared 
|> > |> 	to the Alpha  B-)
|> > 
|> > 
|> > The Sun System is the third fastest measured system in the world, 
|> > the DEC system the second fastest, and the NCR system the 
|> > fastest, on this benchmark.  In absolute performance, the 
|> > NCR system is ahead- but at a price.  It is interesting that 
|> > the NCR system can marshall such a large number of processors 
|> > to work effectively on a benchmark of this type.
|> > 
|> 
|> 
|> 	The NCR "system is ahead- but at a price" is a bit of an understatement
|> 	as the NCR configuration is $17 million+ while the Digital config
|> 	is $1.3 million+.


True.  But, if you happen to really need the power of that system 
at that particular time, and it happens to be the only system 
available to you at that time, it could be worth that much to you...
That is, again, why availability date is so important.

Also, wrt Tandem, Tandem systems have always been directed
towards "high availability", so, one would expect to pay a
premium for that.  If high availability is a *requirement*
for your business/operation, that certainly gets factored
in to the price you are willing to pay for a system.



|> 	If recent history be a guide, Digital will be doing a 4 node 8400 with 
|> 	Memory Channel clustering that should blow that NCR system out of the 
|> 	water like when they recently smashed the former Tandem world-record 
|> 	TPC-C mark.

It could be.  Right now, AFAIK, an 8400 cluster holds the world's record
on TPC-C.  Do you have a schedule for when the appropriate system
will be available (hardware and software)?  I'm not trying to pick
nits, but, "next year", everything will be different, including
Oracle, Sybase, the compilers, the OS, etc.

|> > In short, while the 167 MHz UltraSPARCs are only (very roughly) 
|> > about 1/2 as fast as the 437 MHz Alphas, given their respective
|> > announced availability dates, the gap seems small at the moment.
|> 
|> 
|> 	The 8400 5/440 is available today, while true you would have
|> 	to wait until next year to run the software to get these TPC-D
|> 	marks, 

Just need the software, eh?  ;-)


                the secondary purpose/point of my post is that the UltraSparc
|> 	is truly under-powered compared to the Alpha.  

Hey, I don't own any stock in Sun, or DEC.  
But, "truly under-powered" for the #3 system
in performance and the #2 system in price/performance?  
I don't want to hear what words you would use 
to describe HP.  ;-)

Just for the sake of argument, if HP came out with a
CPU twice as fast as the Alpha servers, would you
say that the Alpha-based system was "truly under-powered"?

Me, I'm amazed that you can get a "PC-like" system 
[with enough disks, etc. etc.] with around 1000-6000 tpmC.  
1500 tpmC used to be a respectable big-system number.  
Now we are seeing big-system numbers in the 10000-30000 tpmC 
range.



                                                       TPC-D more
|> 	than confirms this.  You can argue against Spec but TPC is
|> 	much more difficult to deflect as it is more real-world.  Now
|> 	before you go throwing tomatoes, what I mean by that is that it
|> 	truly exercises an entire system and while Spec95 improved 
|> 	immensely on cache "cheating" there is still consistent threads
|> 	throughout these newsgroups "how come machine X (which is 2 years
|> 	old) performs much better on my application than machine Y, according 
|> 	to Spec machine Y should be much faster".
|> 
|> 	TPC tends to deflate such narrow arguments as the expense and
|> 	size gives you a "truer" indication of underlying server capability.

I agree somewhat, but, the fact that the TPC benchmarks have 
been scaling better recently with respect to total system 
CPU power, and the fact that it is now possible to scale up
onto clustered systems, seems to indicate that these benchmarks 
are more "embarrassingly parallel" than was at all apparent, 
say, three years ago.  That is good news from the standpoint 
of database customers, but it also reduces the general 
applicability of the TPC results.  They are still very good
overall system level benchmarks, and are good tests of
OS robustness, but, seem to be not as good a test of 
"how big a trailer" the system can "pull".



|> 	The gap between UltraSparc and Alpha is significant and will grow
|> 	much larger next year (with the 21264).

Unless Sun comes out with a new-and-improved "UltraSparc 3",
which, *as far as I know*, they might.  Or, maybe SGI/MIPS
will get back on top.  Or, HP.  Or, IBM, Or, x86.  Or, ...


|> 	Would be nice if someone charted announced and available to
