>In article <322EEC47.1C74@sensor.com>, Ron Natalie <ron@sensor.com> writes:
>>I'll say.  If you want to hack UNIX, why deal with these antiques.
>>You can buy a Pentium system put together running Linux out of
>>the box for under $3000 that will run faster and have better
>>graphics than old VAX's or 68000's ....

>And you will get rotten hardware, never according to any real standard, 
>you will never be able to get identical replacement for any part, no
>documentation to speak of and all the other "features" of typical
>dirt cheap PCish hardware. Been there, suffered through it. ....

I would say this is rather extreme.  I have a completely stable 486
system here that I have run NetBSD on (I am using it as I type this),
that I have been running for maybe 2 years now.  I have had essentially
none of the troubles you describe, and am absolutely convinced that the
best deal for the money is the x86 architecture running NetBSD or linux
or the free operating system of your choice.

Not that I don't love the old suns (otherwise, why am I posting in this
newsgroup, but your typical sun3 is 2 or 3 mips which is dog slow,
whereas a high end 486 will crank 40-50 mips and puts most aging sparcstations
to shame.  It is beginning to look like my sun3 systems will become
X-terminals, they are really cost-effective (i.e. cheap) ways to get a
nice high-res display.

For the person eager to keep a motley collection of i386, sun3, and sun4
boxes all going, NetBSD is a nice option since you can be running the
same OS on all the different systems --- and source code to boot!

