newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



In article <50unr9$o6t@news1.io.org>, dlm@io.org (Doug Mallory) writes:

>In article <johnw-0709962325130001@ip-pdx19-27.teleport.com>,
>   johnw@assi.com (John Wong) wrote:
>>Will the DLT drive read a tape created on a TZ30 drive?
>
>Yup!

Well, "maybe" is a safer answer.  There are lots of models of DLT
drives.  Discounting the TK50, TK30 and TK70 (which will read a
TZ30 written) tape, there are also:

TZ85/TF85/TAsomething - Yes.
TZ86/TF86/TAsomething - Yes.

TZ87  - Yes.
TZ87N - No.
TZ88  - Yes.
TZ88N - No.

Then there are the other vendors that have taken Digital or Quantum
DLT2000, DLT4000 and DLT7000 drives and used them in various things.
I'd guess that the majority of DLT2000, DLT4000 and DLT7000 drives
won't read TZ30 written tapes because they are the same basic drives
we used in the TZ87N and TZ88N.  The TZ87 and TZ88 (no N) are basically
binned drives that have been filtered out as being able to read the
older tapes.  Being a smaller segment of the population, they will
tend to cost more.  The N versions are the ones that either didn't
pass or weren't tested (*).

Even the "No" is probably shakey.  I'd guess that drive passes if
can reliably read the older tapes.  Marginal ones are skipped.  Today
you might get lucky and be able to read an older tape.  Next week
maybe not.

(*) I would suppose, but have no personal knowledge, that if the
demand for non-N drives is very high all drives will be tested
to find the better ones.  If the demand is low, they may not 
bother with the extra testing if they have enough from the testing
they've done.
--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




