> Also, wrt Tandem, Tandem systems have always been directed
> towards "high availability", so, one would expect to pay a
> premium for that.  If high availability is a *requirement*
> for your business/operation, that certainly gets factored
> in to the price you are willing to pay for a system.

I thought at least earlier versions of Tandem systems had been
not just "high availability" but actually "fault tolerant",
meaning all current transactions would continue without any
need for being restarted in the event of a hardware failure.

Fault tolerance indeed costs a considerable premium, but if
the goal is merely "high availablilty" (you can restart the
transaction within seconds in the event of a hardware failure),
that can be done with just a VMScluster, without custom hardware.
Disaster-tolerant VMSclusters can even take up the slack in
another city, if you have enough money to pay for the phone
lines.

The extent to which various Unix "cluster" offerings may be
providing these features by now is unknown to me, but whatever
those vendors (including DEC) might offer for Unix can likely
be done without custom hardware also.

Larry Kilgallen



