>> 
>> The only thing this might show is that "64 bit computing" (I assume you are
>> referring to the OS's ability to address with 64 bit pointers) is important
>> for Oracle's implementation.  That is a far cry from showing that 64 bit
>> computing is a win for all applications.  Given Oracle's voracious appetite
>> for shared memory segments, I can see how this might provide better results
>> over buffer caching.
>> 
>
>	Oracle claims their own cache management is far superior to
>	filesystem caching.  This is reflected most apparently in their
>	superior TPC-C results on Digital 8000 series machines.  Sybase

It is generally true of all the RDBMS products that they use their own 
implementation of a buffer cache. I can think of a couple of specific 
reasons for this:
	- Use of a write-ahead-logging protocol requires explicit and
	  precise control of the cache pages, much more so than is available
	  using an OS implementation of VM
	- Being able to understand SQL allows the systems to often know 
	  far in advance exactly what pages will be required, allowing
	  significant performance gains through various types of prefetching

A system designed to perform one task can usually be optimized over a more 
general implementation. For this same reason most RDBMS systems use raw
devices for maximum performance, even though general purpose file systems
provide equivalent functionality.

But with respect to 64 bit systems, I think there is little doubt that 
64 bit systems combined with falling memory prices allow significant 
performance advantages for large RDBMS installations, regardless of the 
RDBMS vendor (assuming that RDBMS vendor has a 64 bit product).

Pat



