Given the nature of CMOS microprocessors:
	a) It's hard to do much about anything until 2001 or so, i.e.,
	because, after all, major microprocessors tend to track major
	chip technology generations.
Figuring out exactly what to do takes months of work from
some of the best engineers we have, 
talking to key customers, and getting better predictions on exactly what
technology we'll have 5 years from now  ... hence, people who believe that
we should instantly & already have detailed specs on exactly what is
going to happen ... are doomed to disappointment.   I can't blame people for
wanting this, but I would observe that the combined company has some
serious engineers working pretty hard on figuring out what to do, and
sending teams (like Chief Scientists, VP's engineering, etc to major
customers to get their input).

I will point out, that in the microprocessor world, and with MIPS chips
in particular, people have often built rather different memory systems
around the same CPU, sometimes in the same package, sometimes in
different packages for different price points.
It ought to be plausible that anybody motivated to
do so, who also owns their CPU technology, might choose to build a
microprocessor with more memory bandwidth (better do this, no matter
what else you're doing), and vector-style capabilities.
After all, the biggest difference between scalable DSMs and vector
systems, over the long term, is in the kind of memory system you
