In article <3235D7E5.167EB0E7@cirl.uoregon.edu>, Tania Bedrax-Weiss wrote:
>I'm really tempted to buy an Alpha 21066-166Mhz but don't have any idea
>of how they perform (compared to an Intel) or if they had had any
>hardware problems.  Anyone with experience in this system?

I'm typing away at the console of one right now.  I *think* I'm telnetted
into another machine, but it doesn't much matter.

It's about comparable to a 75-100MHz Pentium, as far as integer operations
are concerned.  If you're talking Multia/UDB, it's got a fast SCSI
controller, decent Ethernet, and other generally nice stuff on board.

The question really would be what you want to do with it.

It looks like a nice platform to run a web server on; install Linux and
Apache, and you've got a compact, fairly fast box with a goodly amount of
memory that can disappear into the back room.

There's a Linux-Alpha mailing list, if that seems relevant to you.

I've got a web page on the topic:
http://www.conline.com/~cbbrowne/alpha.html

I can't speak for NT or OSF/1 support; for the Multia Alphas, OSF/1 aka
Digital UNIX is not officially supported, and I have no contact with NT.
(Happily.)

The typical $600-800 for these boxes seems reasonable.  They're not
incredible "barn burners," but there's enough on board that it's certainly
not a big rip-off.
