In article <322FC23A.2434@infilsec.com>, Matt Barrie <matt@infilsec.com> writes:
>
>I'm trying to get a DEC5000/150 to boot, and I am having 
>major problems doing so.
>
>
>2 & 3 are the RZ23's borrowed
>from another DEC) 0 is a RZ23L which I think is slightly bigger.

Neither the RZ23 nor RZ23L is large enough for an ULTRIX system
disk.  A root file system and page/swap space, but not a whole
system disk (/usr and space for /var).

>The problem is this: in the DEC manual for the 5000/240 it says to boot
>from a disk drive to use boot 3/rz0/vmunix (or similar, where rzX
>corresponds to the SCSI ID).

If I remember correctly, the Model 150 CPU is for the Model 100
family using a 50 Mhz R4000.  I hope that Model 150 CPU went in
a Model 100 system.  Aside from that, the Model 100 and Model 240
boot syntax is the same.  I think they even had the built-in SCSI
adapter on the same TURBOchannel number.

>
>?IO: 3/rz2/vmunix (bb rd)

I think this is a failure to read the boot block, probably an
I/O error.  Do you know that rz2 works?

>I *think* that maybe it can't find vmunix on any of the disks, and
>that is the reason why it fails to boot before going over to try the
>network. I tried unix, ultrix etc as a test and the same thing happens.

Even if /vmunix hadn't been there you'd have gotten a message
from the loader, /ultrixboot, identifying the version and date
it was built.  The other common choice is /genvmunix.

>Is it possible that the kernel is called something other than vmunix?

What version of ULTRIX do you think you're trying to boot?  I think
you need V4.3A or later to support the R4000 upgrades.

Are you sure one of the RZ23s or RZ23 is bootable?  It isn't large
enough to be a system disk.

Since you can apparently boot from a server, see if you can mount
the file systems you think are on those disks.  The disks are
accessible even with the system booted.  And even without UFS
support built into the kernel programs like fsck and ncheck can
read the file systems.  If diskless systems really expected to
be diskless, a system manager may have elected not to include
UFS support in the kernel.  If you want to mount the file systems
you will need UFS support.
