>and Tandem systems would be already shipping, so someone inclined
>to skepticism about shipping dates would probably want to confirm
>whether or not it is true, and, what the actual shipping dates were.

Let me prepend this note by saying I don't intend any slight
towards either DEC or Sun. My comments are general.

However, this shipping date thing is the single biggest problem
with the TPC benchmarks. Why? The TPC only requires the vendor
sell what it has benchmarked at most 6 months after the benchmark
results are made public. What this means is that the DBMS and
systems companies have entered a mode where new machines/releases
are benchmarked way before the actual hardware/software can be
bought. If the hw/sw being benchmarked is not available 6 months
after the benchmark date, the benchmark must be withdrawn. Big 
deal! At the rate the benchmark numbers climb, a 6 month old
number is usually so out of date nobody cares any longer.

So in actuality, there is no guarantee that the companies are
even benchmarking code a customer can buy! And unlike SPEC,
the TPC benchmarks are unbelievably complex, requiring millions
of dollars of hardware and software and months of work by people
with significant expertise in the area so no customer will ever
be able to prove they cannot duplicate the benchmark numbers 
with over-the-shelf hw and sw.

The best thing the TPC could do, in my opinion, would be
to change the rules so that companies may only benchmark
sw and hw which is actually shipping. Anything else invites
dishonesty.

Pat



