>
>For example, what would be appropriate PM for a MicroVAX
>in a BA123 box?   In a BA213?   An AlphaServer 1000?
>A desktop Alpha?
>
>In the "Good Old Days", Field Service (DEC or otherwise) would
>come out monthly and spend an hour or so making sure everything
>was in proper order.   They would do such things as:

>  - Clean or replace dirty air filters.

If there are any filters, or vents, in the equipment, it would still
be a good idea to do this.

>  - Vacuum dust from the inside of printers, around fans, etc.

It seems perfectly sensible to do this.

>  - Clean and lubricate sliding parts in printing terminals, etc.

Do this for moving parts, such a any door hinges, etc.

>  - Check and adjust voltages.

It's still a good idea to do this every so often.

>  - Verify that all fans were working.

Still needs to be done.  If they're not working, or not spinning
freely, disassemble, clean, lubricate and reassemble them.

>  - Chat with the operator to find out if there had been any
>    unusual sounds, etc. that might indicate problems.

Still makes sense to do so.  It also might not be a bad idea to make
sure that there are no burning smells, sightings of smoke, etc. :-)

Additional things to check:

- Inspect power cords amd make sure that the screws in the plugs are
  tight.  It's no fun to smell a power plug melting.

- Clean thick accumulations of dust off of circuit-boards, etc. 

- Get rid of any spiders.

>Today, equipment is much more compact and reliable.   But is
>there still a need for regular PM?   Is it better to just let

I don't see why there wouldn't be.  Granted, much newer equipment is
nowhere near as nice as much older equipment, and it's certainly not
as repairable with easily obtainable ICs, etc.  However, this is all
the more reason to be careful with it.

