>What constitutes "Preventative Maintenance" today?
>
>For example, what would be appropriate PM for a MicroVAX
>in a BA123 box?   In a BA213?   An AlphaServer 1000?
>A desktop Alpha?
>
>In the "Good Old Days", Field Service (DEC or otherwise) would
>come out monthly and spend an hour or so making sure everything
>was in proper order.   They would do such things as:
>
>  - Clean or replace dirty air filters.
>  - Vacuum dust from the inside of printers, around fans, etc.
>  - Clean and lubricate sliding parts in printing terminals, etc.
>  - Check and adjust voltages.
>  - Verify that all fans were working.
>  - Chat with the operator to find out if there had been any
>    unusual sounds, etc. that might indicate problems.

Wait, Alan, you forgot all the other things that they did
in the "Good Old Days".  Like take your data packs with important
data and erase them, unplug cables and plug them back in backwards,
and change your unit plugs around.  Oh, they'd also blame all
your problems on a third-party product that you'd installed
of years ago and worked just fine, but which suddenly quit 
working after that last upgrade
of VMS :-(.

>Today, equipment is much more compact and reliable.   But is
>there still a need for regular PM?   Is it better to just let
>the system sit there until something breaks, or are there
>tasks that can/should be done to make sure the system *stays*
>running?
>
>This question arose when I got a call from a system manager,
>asking about this.   Apparently, their current field service
>vendor thinks that PM consists of cleaning the terminal screens
>and no more.   The system in question is a pair of MicroVAXes
>in BA123 boxes.

There's one thing that ought to be done to all systems with
DEC RD-series MFM drives, and that is to upgrade to a storage system
more modern.  Running a system with MFM drives is just asking
for trouble - while its true that all hard drives will crash
sooner or later, those MFM drives are gonna crash damn sooner
than decent modern drives.  If it's a critical application, CMD
SCSI controllers that do hardware shadowing would be an excellent
choice.

I know the above doesn't fall into what would be traditionally
called "preventative maintenance".  It's more like removing
someone's appendix while in there for some other sort of surgery.
A sort of "active preventative mainetance".  But there's
no way I'd trust my research or my business to DEC MFM drives
when substantially better mass storage systems are so readily
available.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



