Any MFM hard drives still running are living on borrowed time, IMHO.
The MTTF (mean time to failure) of good RD53's that are running
currently is less than 1 year, in my experience.  RD54's do a little
better.  And when they fail, the data is often unrecoverable.  That's
why I recommend that such systems be replaced by modern SCSI storage
systems, especailly ones that support hardware shadowing.  (You see,
I don't automatically trust SCSI drives with claimed 1000000 hour
MTBF's either.  Any installation where downtime costs money needs
shadowing - because the disk *will* fail just when you need it most.)

>using their ST-506 interface hard drives, as the more people who
>replace them, the more working drives will show up as "For sale" or
>"Free to a good home" for those of us who rely upon them in older
>systems for which no adapter boards for other types of drives are
>available.

Replacing broken RD53's and RD54's as they die isn't very economical
either.  Prices for same-day replacement drives in the LA area
was about $250 for RD53's and $400 for RD54's.  Now it's certainly
true that these drives can be found substantially cheaper if you
shop around and are willing to wait a couple days to get them - but
few applications I know of can live that long without a system disk.
As a result, the most effective "preventative maintenance" for sites
that must still run RD-series drives is to keep several spare drives on site
(often already reformatted and with a image copy of the current
OS on the system disk) for replacement when necessary.  

>What I don't understand is why no one appears to have bothered to push
>the limits of removeable disk pack technology and come up with 14",
>RL02 sized, disk packs, which can hold a gigabyte or two.  This way,
>we wouldn't be stuck with all of these confounded Winchester drives
>that require a clean room for repairs.

Huh?  The only reason RL01's and 02's are so reliable is because the
head flies so high compared to modern hard drives.  (OK,
the embedded servo doesn't hurt either).  And flying a head that
high greatly reduces the storage capacity.  If you tried flying the
heads low enough to get modern data densities, you'd end up needing
to have a clean room to run the drives and store all the packs in.

IMHO, it'll be a while till the Jury decides on the long-term
reliability of "Jaz" and other modern high-density removable drives.
My gut feeling is that they'll fail just as quickly and catastrophically
as the DEC RC25 drives did when operated in real-world environments.
I certainly don't recommend them for anything but non-critical applications.

Tim. (shoppa@triumf.ca)



