In article <50sapo$p9i@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca>,
Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:
>In article <50s59i$3sj@access4.digex.net>,
>R. D. Davis <rdd@access4.digex.net> wrote:

>Replacing broken RD53's and RD54's as they die isn't very economical

However, it's not always necessary to replace a drive which has
failed.  One should first try to determine if the problem is
electrical or mechanical.  If electrical, one then needs to determine
if the problem is inside or outside of the sealed part of the drive.
If the problem's outside, then hopefully it can be traced to an easily
replaceable component (one for which a replacement can be found).
Unfortunately, schematics for most 5-1/4" (and smaller) hard drives
are not easy to find.

Of course, if small, inexpensive, portable "clean room" type units
were available, as well as replacement platters and heads for such
hard drives, things would be greatly simplified.

