In article <1996Sep4.171424.1@eisner>, Rob Young <young_r@eisner.decus.org> wrote:
>
>	The 8400 is best in class for a single SMP system:

These are very good numbers indeed; but in comparing with the Sun test:

. The Sun benchmark was run 5 months previous to the DEC benchmark. At the time,
  the Sun machines were running a 512k ecache; the DEC machine is 4MB.  Sun is 
  now shipping the 1MB ecache, and soon larger.  I wonder how this might affect
  the results (I've never been involved in running TPC benchmarks, so I can't
  speculate)?

. As for the $/QphD figures; I notice the Sun machine was configured with 5.4GB
  of memory while the DEC had 2GB.  This is a price differential of about
  $340,000 (list prices).  One could, of course, argue that the Sun
  configuration needed the extra RAM to achieve better performance (allowing
  higher interleaving rates?), I don't know.  Also, the Sun test was done
  using SPARCstorage arrays, which have quite a few nice features that bare
  4.3GB disks don't - this may have contributed to the price differential
  somewhat as well.

I'm not trying to slight the DEC results, but benchmarks always make me
uneasy.

>	Just goes to show how truly under-powered the UltraSparc is compared 
>	to the Alpha  B-)

No doubt; but, I suspect most people are concerned with the overall
performance of a *system*, not a processor, when benchmarking large
databases.  If I have a system that can deliver a specific performance at a
reasonable price; I'm not likely to care whether I have 12 or 1200
processors in that system (provided that I don't have to expend extra effort
to parallelize my application, or to maintain those 1200 processors).  

As for the 8400 being the best-in-class SMP; I seem to recall that Sun has
recently purchased the Cray Superserver division.  I also seem to recall
that this division was working on an Ultrasparc server as a follow-on to
