newsgroups: vmsnet.pdp-11,alt.sys.pdp11,comp.sys.dec


rdd@access1.digex.net (R. D. Davis) writes:


>Here's how I've connected the cabling:

> TU58 connector 
>----------------

>  A B    
>  C D 
>    F
>  H J
>  K L

>Where: A = auxilary B (not used)
>       B = ground (connected to D)
>       C = receive line + (connected to DLV11J pin 3)
>       D = receive line - (connected to B)
>       F = ground (not connected)
>       G = transmit line - (not connected)
>       H = transmit line + (connected to DLV11J pin 8)
>       K = ground (connected to DLV11J pin 2)
>       L = auxiliary A (not used)


>DLV11J connector 
>----------------

> 10 9    
>  8 7 
>    5
>  4 3
>  2 1 
>       
>Where: 10 = Fuse +12V
>        9 = signal ground (connected to pin 7)
>        8 = received data + (connected to TU58 pin J)
>        7 = received data - (connected to pin 9)
>        5 = signal ground (not connected)
>        4 = signal ground (not connected)
>        3 = transmitted data (connected to TU58 pin C)
>        2 = signal ground (connected to TU58 pin K)
>        1 = UART clock (not connected)


>When I try entering a "dir dd0:" or a "dir dd1:", nothing happens; the
>system just hangs.

>Does the above cabling appear to be correct?

Now, my printsets for the TU58 and the DLV11-J are at home, but from 
memory, I seem to recall that you have to link : 

1) The grounds betweem the 2 devices 
2) RxD+ on both devices to ground
3) RxD- on each device to TxD- on the other one. On the DLV11-J, TxD is 
equivalent to TxD-
4) Leave TxD+ unconnected.

If that doesn't work, get back to me, and I'll dig out the printsets and 
see what is going on. I assume : 

1) The DLV11-J works, and you've set the address and vector to the value 
used by your OS for the DD: driver.
2) The TU58 works. If you're not sure, remove the baseplate (or whatever 
- it depends a bit on the exact model), and observe the red LED on the 
controller board inside. It should flash once (I think it's off-on-off) 
when you turn the machine on. If the TU58 passes its internal self test 
(indicated by that LED), then it should be able to talk to the host.

>Thanks very much in advance for any inforation that anyone can provide
>about this!

