Here's my take on the Cray-SGI merger.
SGI wants, and should rightly be at the forefront of computer technology
devlopment.  Cray was working on something very, very nifty, but their
market fell out as the government tightened up and Power Challenge
Arrays became faster at lower prices. 

Silicon is pretty neat, but Gallium Arsenide is much faster (and pretty
much impossible to make a chip out of.....) Cray was working on GA parts
as of a few years ago, but I think they lost the funding...

So, I think that SGI bought Cray's GA research, and we'll see a GA based
challenge/Onyx, possibly incorporating optical busses/DSPs as well as
holographic memory in the not-too-distant future.


Oh, SGI dosen't want to "kill" Cray, it's much easier (and makes for
better computers) if they work together as one.

Note that these predictions have no real factual basis and if you go and
loose $1,000,000 you can't sue me over it :)

Cheers!
Ben.



