>MIPS processor line, and NEC is able to add value and create the
>world's fastest supercomputers with them.  Meanwhile SGI acquired CRAY
>and is competing with NEC on the high end.  Hmmm.

I hope that you are not thinking that NEC uses the R10000 in the SX-4.
The SX-4 cpu has nothing to do with the R10000, and as far as I can
tell the SX-4 cpu was designed specifically for the SX-4.  It looks to
me like a 2-wide statically scheduled superscalar CMOS processor
running at 125 MHz.  Not too exciting for 1996....

In contrast, the R10000 is a 4-wide, dynamically scheduled, out-
of-order processor running at 175-195 MHz (depending on the exact
product).

The scalar performance of the NEC SX-4 should not be in the same league
as the R10000 (SPECint95 on the SX-4, anyone?), while the R10000
clearly cannot be attached to the SX-4 memory system and 8 vector pipes
per cpu.

These are completely different sorts of critters --- so different that
it would be really hard to figure out a "reasonable" way of comparing
them, other than pointing out how different they are in price and
performance characteristics.
