   ----- Transcript of session follows -----
While talking to smtp.teleport.com:
>>> RCPT To:<tashman@teleport.com>
<<< 550 <tashman@teleport.com>... User unknown
550 <tashman@teleport.com>... User unknown


the RT11-SJ o/s runs on PDP-11s so you might want to try one of the
groups that focus on them, like comp.sys.dec.micros or vmsnet.pdp-11
(I believe a few of the old PDP folks still hang out there).

sorry I can't help with any definite info, I wasn't an RT11 guy.
offhand I'd be pessimistic about the prospects of finding a
cross-compiler for Intel or most any other architecture.  A lot of the
issues will depend on whether you've got the source files - if not,
cross compilation won't help without first running a dis-assembler
against the machine language executables, and that's likely to be a
real can of worms!  If you have the sources, and they are most likely
MACRO-11 assembler (the other popular language for RT was Fortran),
then a cross-compiler would help, and if it's a high-level language it
could probably be ported without too much trouble.  Even an assembler
language program might not be too bad, depending on the specifics of
the application.

You might also try the decus newsgroup - I'm not subscribed to that
one here, so can't give you the full correct name, sorry.  There might
be some s/w in the decus library that would be helpful to you, and
there are likely to be some PDP-11 folks still around there too.

Good luck!

--bruce mcculley
  ex-RSX11 developer



