In article <50bckp$9a5@paraguay.earthlink.net>, luke@us1a.msrcnavo.navy.mil writes:

> Most people I know are pretty worried about Cray's viability after the
> takeover. SGIs attitude seems to be "We killed 'em good, let's keep it
> that way and sell more tweaked graphics boxes."  There's just not
> enough volume in it for them with supercomputers that differ greatly
> from their graphics workstation line, is there?

That seems an odd view. 

The people at SGI (including Cray people) feel just the opposite. If
we'd "killed 'em good and wanted to keep it that way", we could have
saved $700M by not purchasing the company. It must be clear to all that
we invested in Cray because we respected what they are good at and are
*confident* (signing a $700M check is the definition of "Damn Sure")
that the two companies are stronger and more innovative working
together than against each other.

It's true that in pre-acquisition times our high-end was doing well
against their low-end, but Cray high-end products have been and remain
outstanding products (even if they're a bit expensive ;-)  It's also
true that the two engineering groups are working well together -- the
mood is a happy one and good ideas are flowing both ways.  It would not
surprise me to see products sharing combined innovations sooner than 
expected.

Volume is not everything, even in companies with considerable unit
sales such as Silicon Graphics. Just as the InfiniteReality *defines*
high-end graphics, Cray's best products *define* HPC. This type of
smaller-volume innovative leadership is a form of "self-financed R&D"
that has worked well for SGI in graphics and is expected to do the 
same in supercomputing. 

[My personal thoughts, entirely, as a lover of technology and one 
who is eager to create the best products that can be built.]

