> A few questions about a Vaxstation 3100 M76 SPX I was given recently.  What
> does the "M76 SPX" mean?

        The "M76" means it's a "Model  76".   There  are about ten or so
    different  models  of VAXstation 3100, starting with the  VS3100/30.
    Usually, the high the model number the faster the machine.  Where  a
    model  30  was  2.8 VUPS (where 1 VUP = VAX 11/780), the model 76 is
    rated at 7.6 VUPS.  [Note: the Microvax 3100 series uses some of the
    same model  numbers,  but  they're  NOT  the  same preformance.  The
    MV3100/30  and MV3100/40 are substantially faster than the VS3100/30
    and VS3100/40...]  The VS3100/76 has SPECint92 of 7.1,  SPECfp92  of
    6.6.

>                           Looks to have a substantial graphics card in it - I 
> presume that's the "SPX" bit?  I have no monitor for it so can anyone tell me 
> the pinout of the monitor connector or else can I plug in a terminal and boot 
> using that?  Any comments on what kind of performance I can expect (yeh - I 
> know it'll be slower than an Alpha !!) .

        The "SPX" is market-speak  if  you  ask  me, like "GTO" (oooops,
    just  dated  myself  :-).  I've heard it  translated  as  "Scan-line
    Processor eXtension", or something like that.  It does 8-plane, 1280
    by 1024 graphics.  I don't know the other monitor requirements other
    than the usual DEC "sync on green".  You can  pick  up  a  used  DEC
    VRT19-DA  (19" color) monitor for a these for a few hundred dollars.
    (In fact, we recently  upgraded  10  monochrome VS3100/76's to SPX's
    for  about $500US per workstation, which included the VRT19-DA _and_
    the SPX graphics board.  Check the used DEC  equipment  dealers  for
    prices.)

