Hi Mitch--

Gawd, I hate essay questions! True-False or multiple-guess questions are
much easier to answer. Since your questions can't be addressed so
succinctly, they are indeed **good** questions. Let me take a stab at some
of them...

> Terry,
> 
> I find your news letter quite interesting and because you make an
> effort to keep up to date with the inner workings of DEC perhaps you
> could outline the next series of organizations Bob intends to
> eliminate. Lets see--it can't be disks, monitors, printers
> nor could it be "home" PCs, Disaster Recovery, Rdb or Alta Vista
> because they are already gone. 

With the exception of AltaVista, you are dead on. AltaVista is now a
subsidiary. I strongly believe that DEC will maintain an Internet
presence--they'd be nuts not to do so. 

Perhaps he will deep six what is ever
> left of the SI or the NI business (if any thing is left after the
> current small incremental lay off of 7,000). 
> 

SI and NI have been merged into the new Digital Services Division. Drop
those capabilities, and there goes the bulk of the firm's value
proposition as well as a key differentiator. On the other hand, I would
expect to see some reductions in the DSD over time.

The question of where do the 7K reductions come from is a good question
indeed.  If DEC were to deep-six the entire PCBU (which ain't gonna
happen) they'd cut the workforce by no more than 3K. I **do** believe that
the Components and Peripherals Business Unit will be eliminated from the
org chart. After all, the only components and peripherals that remain
within the portfolio are memory and printers, and there's a good chance
that both of those will go.

On the subject of disks, you are right, DEC no longer manufactures same.
They do, however, integrate commodity components under the StorageWorks
label. StorageWorks, which recently was folded into the Systems Business
Unit, has 1,400 employees and a $1.4 billion annual run rate. And it's
**very** profitable. Matter of fact, in FY95 StorageWorks posted a net
profit of $128M while DEC's total profit for the year was $124M.

> How long before DEC's population will be BELOW 30,000?
> 

I can make only one observation in this regard. More than two years ago, I
went on record with the statement that DEC's ultimate headcount goal was
"at or below 50K." On a revenue per employee basis, the only way DEC can
justify a 60K headcount is with $21B in revenue (quote from Palmer, but I
forget the source). Based on annual revenue of $14B, it looks like 40K is
a reasonable long-term figure.

> How long before large numbers of existing customers (perhaps as many
> as 80%) will be serviced by TPM�s like DecisionOne rather than by
> Digital Field Service - a matter of months?
> 

It depends. DEC has made it quite clear that it wants to get out of the
traditional break/fix business. Hence, the Authorized Warranty Service
Provider's Program.

> How long before what remains of sales both Hardware and Services will
> be deemed expendable (I.E.)  How long before DEC becomes another DELL
> or Gateway ?

If DEC becomes another Dell or Gateway, DEC is toast. Note that Gateway is
looking into expanding its distribution strategy beyond telephone or mail
order. While Digital must more effectively exploit indirect channels, an
indirect-only strategy could be endorsed only by Dr. Kevorkian.

> 
> As for a replacement -- Gerstner (or at least some one like him). IBM
> not only had (and in some cases still has) the same problems as DEC
> yet IBM stock continues to grow in value. Gerstner does more for IBM
> that just to lay off people and reorganize. I would be interested
> (again because you are closer than most) by your count how many major
> reorganizations has Bob devised over the last five years 10 - 20?  

Like yourself, I've lost count. Recall, however, that there was a whole
lotta of reorgin' goin' on during the one-year tenure of Ed Lucente.
Reorgs during the Spring '93 to Spring '94 timeframe are best attributed
to Ed. As for myself, I've characterized DEC's structure as "The Reorg
That Wouldn't Die" more than once!

>I would also be interested in the current number of VP's - what is the
> ratio of "VP to Worker" and how does that compare with HP ? 

I can't answer that. I **do** know that more than 75 percent of the senior
management team has been replaced under Palmer.

> One more statistic (if you know) how many body guards does Bob have?

Not as many as he had in Spring 1994! 
> 
> In short, Bob needs to move on !!!!
> 

That may be so. I suspect that if DEC does not return to profitability by
the end of the calendar year, you'll find that your statement is heavily
supported by opinion polls!



