All this is true, but of course you can load the OS thru a network.

>The good news is that your machine may be upgradable to a SPARC based
>system (the first SPARC's were Sun 3's with a SPARC VME processor
>card). If you can find a source (look in the SUN newsgroups) this
>would allow you to run Solaris and give a performance boost.

Eh? Excuse me?  All the Sun-3's are 68000 machines.  Indeed you could
"upgrade" the 3/80 to a sparcstation, but this involves keeping the case
and power supply and dropping a whole sparcstation 1 or 1+ board into
it.  (If you do this, let me know and I'll take that 3/80 board off
your hands).

What would I do if I were in your shoes?  I would make my life simple
and probably follow option 1 (but I have a personal inclination towards
Sun software as well as hardware).  If you have a personal preference
for DEC things you might take a different course.  NetBSD is an option
but at this time the port for the 3x architecture is "in the works".
The 3/80 is a 3x class machine since it has a 68030 with the on-chip
MMU rather than the sun-mmu in the rest of the sun3's.  One angle on
NetBSD is that you may be able to run it on the 3100 right now (but I
am not certain of this), and someday the 3/80 as well.

