Call me naive, but I thought $700M was a pretty good deal for Cray,
made available by an opportune moment in their product development
cycle.  It seems their business backlog and projected earnings on the
T3E line should bolster the stock, making the $700M "price"
irrelevant.  The only money flowing out of the door would be that paid
to the executive team at Cray upon their acceptance of the deal.

It seems that SGI got a killer deal and got rid of the competition in
what used to be called the minisuper market and is rapidly becoming
the mainsuper market.  The Challenge series and the J90 were neck and
neck.  Additionally, the next generation products from both companies
were so similar, that a predatory deal was the perfect move for SGI.

Now, the tricky question: Will SGI take advantage of their position
and give the Cray folk sufficient autonomy to continue to reinvent the
supercomputer?  Is there a bonafide market for supercomputers that
will allow SGI to do this?

