Tony Konashenok (tonyk@sseos.lbl.gov) wrote:
: Does anyone know if...

: 1 ...KA650/MS650 support battery backup through appropriate Q-bus pins?

: 2 ...it is possible to "organize" battery backup on a VS3100? (Obviously,
:      in an unsupported and undocumented way).

: 3 ...VMS would live on such a machine (either (1) or (2)), i.e. it would
:      be possible to boot VMS, turn main power off with backup being on,
:      turn power back on, and continue as if nothing had happened? If not,
:      is there any other VMS platform that would, and does not cost big
:      bucks?

VMS has a built in concept called power failure recovery.  It is meant to
recover from a power failure when memory-only battery backup is used.

Power failure recovery depends on the CPU recognizing the power failure 
interrupt, then responding according to the console setting (which can
behalt, reboot, restart, others depending on the model of the VAX).

Power failure recovery also depends on the ability of peripheral devices to
re-establish a useable state, which depends not only on the design of the 
peripheral hardware, but also the coding of the device driver.  Not all
peripherals will successfully restart, but most disk drives will.

