LUKE -- You are right in highlighting the "religious differences" that
surface when one company acquires another, and that spill over into their
business models. The process of mutual religious deprogramming has come
along surprisingly far already (with joint technical and marketing teams
leading the way).  Specifically:

*There is solid agreement (and a plan) to create a common OS that combines
the high-end capabilities of UNICOS with IRIX.  All recognize the need for
maintaining a "high-end design center" in HW/SW offerings.  You'll see
evidence of this in the product code-named "Lego" (aka "SN-0") that comes
even before SN-1, and of course in the continuing Cray products. 

*There's also solid commitment (plans) to include vector capabilities in
the unified Cray-SGI products.  That's slated for timeframe of what you
call SN-2, circa year 2000, with existing Cray vector products being
enhanced in the meantime.  Exact timing of plan depends not only on how
fast it CAN be done technically, but also what makes sense from customers'
standpoints--i.e., expect to see some phase-in periods to accommodate
needs of different customers. 

*SGI's a big organization and there may be people who have the "we killed
Cray and who needs large-scale supercomputers" attitude, but the
decision-makers (management,technical,sales) at SGI don't think that way.
They understand Cray's market is largely different from SGI's and
different capabilities are needed for high-end customers (just how
different has become clearer since the merger).  Anyway, the rationale for
the merger wasn't "buy-and-kill"...a high price to pay if starting
assumption had been SGI already beat Cray in the marketplace.  Remember
SGI was already trying to move upmarket over time--for access to high-end
customers, which include large, prestigious names in gov't, industry,
academia, and for the jump-start those customer relationships give to
technical innovation that can be exploited in lower-cost, higher-volume
markets.  Easiest route was to buy rather than try to build high-end
supercomputing capabilities.

Not sure I answered your questions altogether, but hope this is a start.
Best, Steve

