Luke Lonergan <luke@us1a.msrcnavo.navy.mil> wrote:
>
>Vector at SGI? Really? Will SN-1 or 2 have vector? I've heard it both
>ways, but it sounds less probable as time progresses...

Does it make any difference?  (And, no, I don't know the answer to
the original question.)

(One difficulty of being on the inside of a company now is that I
can't comment on future things that I know anything about.  So if you
see me posting here, it is only because I know nothing about what SGI
is doing in a particular area.  After I have been here a bit longer,
I will probably have to shut up entirely.... :-)

The real issue is performance (and I have hammered all the micro-based
vendors on this as hard as anyone), and I think it is safe to say that
we have no intention of giving up on the development of systems with
continually increasing performance.  There are some aspects of the 
performance issues of getting increased memory system throughput that
can be handled by vectors, some by streams, and some by more general
methods.  There was actually a pretty intelligent discussion on these
topics on comp.arch recently....



>Most people I know are pretty worried about Cray's viability after the
>takeover. SGIs attitude seems to be "We killed 'em good, let's keep it
>that way and sell more tweaked graphics boxes."  There's just not
>enough volume in it for them with supercomputers that differ greatly
>from their graphics workstation line, is there?

We have every intention of making money with the Cray division.  We are
moving in this direction by eliminating the need for Cray to expand downward
into the low-end market, and allowing them to concentrate on the larger
systems and the value-added support that they do so well.  Improved 
opportunities to take advantage of the synergies of supercomputing and
high-end graphics are, of course, an additional benefit of the merger.

SGI+Cray now have by far the largest market share in HPC in both the
$100k-$1M range and the $1M+ range (which combine to be a $2B market),
and it would be silly to give those up!

On the other hand, SGI+Cray is currently selling at a rate of over $3B
annually, so it is clear that the high-end arena cannot be the company's
sole focus.  The investment of $760M in Cray Research makes it clear that
it is a *very large* focus, however, and the organization of SGI makes it
quite possible to act on that focus.

The merger plan does look to see the end of the PVP line, as the
microprocessor-based machines catch up to it in performance.  Catching
up is the key point -- there is no sense in retiring the technology until
we can match its performance at a lower cost with a micro-based solution.

All of this is in the corporate roadmap:
	<http://www.cray.com/PUBLIC/product-info/RM/>
